.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd November 4, 2026
.Dt BUILD-BSD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm build-bsd
.Nd build, stage, and package 5BSD through maintained system targets
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm plan
.Cm build | world | kernel | stage | packages | sign | verify | inventory | inventory-audit | reconcile | construct | boot-observe | snapshot | deploy | bsdinstall | media | secureboot-test | bazel-test
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Cm status
.Ar run-directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The command is installed as
.Nm build-bsd ;
.Nm bsd-build
is retained as a compatibility alias.
Both names invoke the same compiled frontend and use the same configuration
and build lock.
Backend commands receive separate arguments, without shell
expansion of configuration values.
.Pp
.Nm
builds the world and selected kernel from
.Pa /usr/src
using every logical CPU by default and the standard
.Pa /usr/obj
object root.
It invokes maintained make targets, records per-step logs, stops on failure,
and serializes its own executions with a build lock.
Independent manual make invocations must not overlap the same object tree.
Build, stage, packages and verify do not install an upgrade on the host.
Deploy requires an explicit alternate destination.
No command activates a
boot environment or reboots the machine.
The bsdinstall command explicitly opens the interactive disk installer; its
disk selections can replace an existing system.
.Pp
The normal make buildworld, buildkernel and packages targets remain supported.
A world intended for package publication must be built with
.Va WITH_MALLOC_PRODUCTION=yes ;
development worlds retain debug malloc by default.
The packages make target produces base packages; system-packages completes the
matching base and hardware publication workflow.
.Nm
orchestrates these targets and the explicit signing and verification actions.
Veriexec support is already compiled into GENERIC; signatures and trust policy
are separate inputs, and using this frontend alone does not enable enforcement.
Secure Boot additionally requires signing keys and firmware trust enrollment.
BIP development is dropped. The experimental GENERIC-INTEGRITY profile and
kernel enforcement implementation are archived and are not supported by the
current source tree. Retained image and storage tooling does not establish a
root-resistant immutable installation. Signed package publication, veriexec
support, and Secure Boot each require separate qualification; none alone
establishes BIP. Historical BIP fixture commands require the archived source.
Later BIP-specific descriptions in this manual are retained design context,
not supported enforcement in the active tree.
.Pp
World and kernel compile only the selected component.
Sign finalizes an existing alternate root with an explicit signing key.
The build command is an alias for packages: it compiles world and kernel
and completes the pkgbase publication pipeline.
Stage is a separate private VM/development operation; it compiles and installs
into a new private NO_ROOT directory, runs
distribution and kernel installation there, and finalizes signing.
Packages defaults to a production world/kernel build and invokes
system-packages,
which checks production malloc and kernel/hardware identity, stages base and
hardware separately, and publishes one combined repository.
World package creation rejects conflicting installation metadata before
sorting METALOG and generating package lists.
Each invocation selects fresh world, kernel and source package stages beneath
its run directory in
.Pa /usr/obj/usr/src/build-runs .
Incremental compilation reuses objects, but never reuses a previously signed
installation stage. Failed stages are retained with their run logs.
The repository remains
.Pa /usr/obj/usr/src/repo ;
base-only output does not advance latest.
.Pp
Plan prints the actions without building, creating directories, generating
keys,
or publishing anything.
Status prints a run's current or final state.
Snapshot verifies and copies a completed signed repository using the maintained
consumer verifier.  It requires
.Fl repository ,
.Fl pkg-public-key ,
.Fl package-tool ,
.Fl snapshot-abi ,
and
.Fl snapshot-osversion .
The copied catalogue retains its signature, and every package archive is checked
against it.  The output must be new under
.Pa /usr/obj ;
by default it is the repository subdirectory of the new build run.
The output parent must already exist when an explicit output is supplied.
Snapshot does not select packages, construct a filesystem, authorize a generation,
or change the published latest pointer.  Consumer trust is an explicit public key;
no private signing key is needed.
.Pp
Verify checks all system bundles in an existing signed root,
using native capsign tools and an explicitly trusted public key.
It does not compile world or kernel, install packages, or execute target code.
Bsdinstall verifies and snapshots a completed signed pkgbase repository before
launching the native interactive installer.
It requires both public trust keys,
a terminal, root privileges, and an installed bsdinstall supporting explicit
repository selection and code verification.
It refuses a foreign architecture
and requires the repository to include pkg, the base set and selected kernel.
The installer verifies signed bundles before boot setup.
Preparation, installer logs and recovery files remain beneath build-runs,
including after failure.
Installer creation and VM validation remain separate
from this launch operation.
Media builds disc1.iso, memstick.img, or both from an existing built
world/kernel
and a completed signed publication.
The maintained release targets check
production malloc and exact live-kernel identity, preserve the complete signed
catalogue, and embed only public trust keys.
A new output directory isolates
release staging from the standard world/kernel objects.
The frontend checks
requested artifacts and writes SHA256/SHA512 checksum files.
It does not boot
an image or launch the installer.
Full image boot/install validation is still
required before this new path is considered release-ready.
Logs reside under
.Pa /usr/obj/usr/src/build-runs .
A failed stage is incomplete and must not be installed or published.
.Pp
The separate maintained
.Pa tools/test/daemon-runtime/run-vm.py
runner accepts explicit world and kernel inputs for confined ZFS mechanism tests
with
.Fl -zfs .
The optional
.Fl -scratch-disk-mib
input creates a new private virtual disk for explicit-device import tests.
It never selects or attaches a host disk or pool.
It records test timeouts and cleanup requirements and rejects missing cleanup
evidence.
These tests do not qualify an installed protected generation and are
not a replacement for the Secure Boot or packaged-system upgrade gates.
.Pp
The tool does not silently resume or reuse an incomplete stage.
.Ss Manifest trust selection
The normal world and loader build accepts
.Va WITHOUT_LOADER_EFI_TRUST_DB=yes
and explicit public X.509
.Va TRUST_ANCHORS
through the source configuration selected with
.Fl -src-conf .
This embeds generation trust in the loader and excludes firmware db certificates
from manifest trust, while retaining firmware revocations.
Supply public certificates only; trust-anchor inputs become loader contents.
The build rejects private-key or mixed PEM framing before embedding.
Certificate decoding and trust verification remain separate checks.
The default continues to import firmware db trust.
Changing trust inputs requires rebuilding the loader and its libraries;
the generated trust header is regenerated to prevent stale anchor selection.
.Pp
This selection does not authorize a generation, implement anti-rollback,
or prevent firmware from selecting another trusted loader.
The default disposable-key secureboot-test fixtures exercise firmware-backed
manifest trust. Explicit generation test credentials select compiled-only trust
for a selected case; actual firmware execution is required to qualify it.
Do not count a loader build or signature check as such qualification.
.Ss Historical BIP and Secure Boot qualification
BIP has been dropped.
The descriptions in this subsection describe the former combined fixture,
not supported BIP options or qualification of the current tree.
The runner cleanup and independent Secure Boot qualification remain incomplete.
.Pp
Packaged extension and device permissions are configuration inputs.
BSDExtension requires its packaged allow-list at startup and has no compiled
module grants; BSDDevice ships an empty device grant list.
These files follow normal package inventory, staging and bundle verification.
Parsing and signing them do not qualify protected runtime storage or policy updates.
.Pp
The staged BSDFilesystem provider requires a bounded storage-root bootstrap grant
under BIP; publication does not establish that the authenticated import and grant
path works end to end or that persistent storage has been qualified.
Capsule's packaged configuration grants no persistent storage by default.
Installations must supply an authenticated
.Va storage
policy with explicit devices, pool/dataset GUIDs, recipient, operation rights
and a stable
.Va hostuuid ;
see
.Xr capsule.conf 5 .
Staging does not discover host pools or invent these deployment inputs.
.Pp
BSD Integrity Protection (BIP) protects the operating system\(aqs integrity
by enforcing restrictions that apply even to root.
Secure Boot and code signing are supporting mechanisms.
The internal kernel policy name remains MAC_SYSTEM_INTEGRITY.
.Pp
Secureboot-test cleans and rebuilds the EFI loader in a private object subtree
below its build-run directory, with firmware trust and
mandatory boot-file verification, rebuilds Capsule and SwitchBoard using the
matching world build environment, builds a static guest fixture, then runs a
networkless QEMU Q35/SMM suite against an existing staged root and matching
kernel objects.
It uses all logical CPUs for compilation.
The bootstrap step also builds the runtime linker, used by both the baseline
probe and Capsule/native-service fixtures. Private stage replacement checks
reject symlink parents/leaves and non-file destinations.
With a BIP kernel, a dynamic
setuid probe checks authenticated-root libmap inputs and rejection of writable
and unrelated read-only replacements, including include files, include
directories and a FIFO. Signals and timeouts do not count as policy rejection.
Library hints are checked through dlinfo, and identical writable or foreign
read-only hints replacements and FIFO substitution must be rejected.
A shared test library must load and execute through name lookup, absolute
paths and fdlopen from the authenticated root; identical writable and foreign
read-only copies, and FIFO substitutions, must be rejected on every route.
An identical non-setuid control must load and execute the same library from
ordinary writable and read-only storage using all three APIs, with real and
effective UID 65534 and no credential taint.
These mechanism checks do not qualify separate service-generation mounts,
production hints provisioning or installed-service startup.
The required signed /boot/lua/secureboot.lua policy reads explicit generation
configuration and boots without an interactive menu or command fallback.
Missing required policy or configuration fails closed.
Unsigned ESP settings, ZFS nvstore settings and optional nextboot/local
overrides cannot supply policy in this mode.
It generates disposable PK, KEK and signing keys outside guest images,
enrolls a private OVMF variable store, and checks approved boot, rejection,
revocation and unauthorized variable changes by guest root.
It never changes host firmware or publishes these test credentials.
.Pp
This action requires native amd64, QEMU with Secure Boot OVMF, OpenSSL,
uefisign, makefs, mkimg, Python 3.12 and virt-fw-vars (tested with virt-firmware
26.10).
Dependencies must already be installed; the command does not install packages.
Use
.Fl -root
for an existing stage under /usr/obj and
.Fl -output
for a new result directory under /usr/obj.
The result.json report records each test and its evidence.
A final retained-evidence step requires complete case coverage and rehashes the
recorded inputs, logs, firmware variables, disk and root images.
For paired provider boots it also checks the storage and firmware handoff.
It writes evidence-verification.json without replacing an existing receipt.
This is a consistency check of trusted-runner evidence, not a signed attestation
or installed-system qualification.
Supplying both storage inputs adds the storage-anchor case to the normal matrix.
Adding
.Fl -filesystem-policy
selects the actual Capsule/SwitchBoard/BSDFilesystem integration case instead.
The protected kernel must include BIP and have a matching built ZFS module.
The bootstrap build also builds the storage fixture with matching world
libraries.
See
.Pa tools/test/secureboot/README.md
for disposable disk preparation and the remaining integration boundaries.
The selected loader is an explicit, hashed runner input.
Firmware-verification compiler options do not alter the shared world objects
or the BIOS loader used by a subsequent incremental publication.
An incomplete run is not a passing qualification.
The fixture boots PID 1 from a whole-image authenticated UFS preload and checks
read-only access plus denial of forced detach and resize.
It also requires root process-debug isolation between separate authority-bearing
executions of the same approved image, while preserving ordinary fork behavior.
The same kernel fixture requires immutable invocation-bound channel checks:
owner use, fork and successful-exec reuse denial, credential independence,
failed-exec preservation and software revocation.
With BIP, the static fixture also requires system-operation authority checks:
ordinary root cannot claim gates, inherit PID 1 owner authority, mint or release
its claims, or bypass delegation through raw clock-write syscalls.
An explicitly delegated clock token must work and lose authority when closed.
The Capsule fixture repeats the root denial checks from its rc child and
requires denial of its unauthorized raw poweroff attempt.
The host then stops that VM after all assertions and manager READY; this cleanup
is reported separately and does not qualify authorized guest shutdown.
These tests do not qualify production time-service or user authorization policy.
The bootstrap backend rebuilds libpam and stages that exact shared library.
Its native fixture also requires PAM module-resolution checks: rejected
candidates stop lookup, absent candidates permit fallback, and explicit numeric
versions select the named module.
It also requires PAM include-cycle, nesting and expansion rejection, while
preserving valid repeated includes, combined-file services and recovery after
failed configuration.
The bootstrap backend also rebuilds pwd_mkdb and runs its maintained account
database tests as UID 65534 in isolated guest scratch directories.
They check incremental UID records, duplicate-UID ownership and rejection of
malformed updates against actual pwd.db and spwd.db contents.
The retired session fixture no longer requires a host account database generator.
This does not qualify complete PAM/NSS/account dependency integrity or a
protected credential-update transaction.
.Pp
The populated native fixture also exercises actual SwitchBoard status channels
and administrative denial for an unprivileged software grant running as UID 0.
The native fixture additionally enrolls a test-only Core issuer to exercise
independent management and lifecycle roles, read-only principals, lease closure,
expiry, cached channels, and software eligibility independently of session rights.
Signed images containing duplicate or embedded-NUL session-policy keys,
unknown session rights, or include directives must reject startup.
Signed images with missing Capsule policy, duplicate or embedded-NUL keys,
unknown fields, invalid field types or include directives must reject before
service startup without entering a recovery shell.
The bootstrap build rebuilds libcapsulert and the image consumes that rebuilt
library so claim-parser changes are included even when using an older stage.
The ordinary world tests include Capsule policy and shared claim-parser checks
for selected inputs, atomic rejection and bounded descriptor parsing.
The Capsule fixture also loads the shipped storage policy through the actual
BSDFilesystem parser from the authenticated read-only root, rejects a mutable
policy descriptor, and requires BIP status reads to work in capability mode
while policy writes remain denied.
The ordinary world test build includes BSDFilesystem config_test for schema,
duplicate, include, type, range, descriptor and selected-input checks.
These checks do not qualify persistent dataset delegation or normal system startup.
The networkless fixture also does not qualify SSH or network-administration
mediation; legacy routing/interface privileges remain an explicit installed-system
acceptance gate.
The maintained bootstrap step rebuilds libservice, pam_unix, and the session
personal-service and principal-provider fixtures.
Two unsigned personal bundles with the same label exercise explicit start,
owner scope, cached management denial after logout, and confinement of requests
for root credentials and Core status. Root and same-UID processes must fail
ambient signal and debugger access to these protected services.
Guest ownership comes from the fixture METALOG; no host account changes occur.
Both personal services must report readiness after checking their delegated
volatile containers: ownership, create/read/fsync/rename/delete and denied
parent/global traversal in capability mode.
Persistent personal storage remains a separate acceptance gate.
The native fixture's rc software grants come from the explicit
policy/boot-authority.ucl test input, whose digest is recorded with the run.
These fixture grants are not installed as production authorization policy.
The protected discovery test also grants the rc probe software eligibility for
the personal endpoint, then requires denial after root changes to each owner's
UNIX UID without a session. The authenticated issuer, still UID 0, must reach
the correct same-named provider for each session, including on-demand activation.
New lookup requests after logout must fail. Already-delivered provider channels
and their duplicates must become unusable immediately after logout and on expiry.
The static kernel fixture also requires independent software and lifetime checks,
queued-message refusal, blocked-receive wakeups, kqueue EOF, immutable binding,
last-duplicate semantics, issuer/control closure, inheritance restrictions,
and repeated lease teardown. These checks run under actual UEFI Secure Boot.
The native fixture loads the shipped 5bsd-authenticate PAM policy and requires
it to deny authentication and account management until an owner supplies
an authenticated protected-account policy.
A separate explicit vm-ordinary-password test policy loads staged PAM modules
against synthetic ordinary account records.
It checks passwords (including hashes of empty passwords), account expiration,
required password changes, rejection of
UID impersonation, and broker denial of authenticated but unenrolled principals.
The fixture supplies its own password conversation and is not a trusted login UI.
.Pp
A separate test policy selects pam_unix's
.Cm protected_passwd
option with an explicit immutable secret credential file.
The fixture checks correct and incorrect passwords, missing protected users,
locked accounts, empty passwords, expiry, and independent session enrollment.
Protected remote-account cases accept numeric IPv4/IPv6 peers and reject
hostnames and invalid addresses; absent or empty peers retain local semantics.
These supplied peer values do not establish a trusted connection origin.
A paired login-class case checks ordinary default fallback and protected
rejection of the same missing class.
A root-owned copy of valid credentials on writable storage must be rejected by
both authentication and account management; protected positive controls follow.
These are synthetic credential tests, not production enrollment or proof that
ordinary account databases may safely become writable.
.Pp
The shipped 5bsd-authenticate policy denies all operations by default.
An installation enabling protected authentication must supply its protected PAM
policy and credential file as explicit,
hash-bound installation-policy inputs, keep login-class policy immutable, and
qualify the resulting complete generation.
The existing installation-policy file creation and replacement mechanism
applies;
using build-bsd alone does not select a credential store or enroll a user.
.Pp
An independent provider fixture checks broker-attested principal metadata,
denied anonymous root access, distinct permissions for principals sharing a UID,
object ownership and denial of software-only provider ADMIN bypass.
The native result requires PROVIDER_PRINCIPAL_PASS.
This qualifies the metadata mechanism and a test operation policy;
production providers still require their explicit operation and resource policies.
The assembler uses the freshly rebuilt libservice for its new session API;
other dependencies come from the matching world stage.
These checks do not establish human authentication, protected login input,
full user-service authorization or production authenticator enrollment.
A kernel built with MAC_SYSTEM_INTEGRITY, such as the experimental
GENERIC-INTEGRITY profile, additionally requires BIP bootstrap root-attack
checks, rejects a mutable preload or disabled Secure Boot, and ignores alternate
root selection. The runner checks the embedded kernel configuration, not just
the configuration name. Plans report bootstrap_integrity_verification as
kernel-dependent; the result records which policy actually ran.
The protected-profile fixture also checks protection at process creation,
before initialization and across exec/fork, explicit process-descriptor lifecycle
control, and core-dump suppression with an ordinary core-dump control.
An additional protected-profile case boots the actual dynamically linked
Capsule and SwitchBoard, with their runtime loader and shared-library closure
copied from the staged world into a whole-image authenticated UFS preload.
A bounded rc probe checks denied root interference and immutable code, then
allows rc to complete. Success requires Capsule's authenticated READY receipt
and EFI shutdown. These baseline cases use an empty catalogue.
A populated case builds and signs the actual BSDAudit and BSDNotify bundles,
using a native host signer and a disposable code key outside the guest.
The maintained stage signing and verification helpers enforce the exact code
trust catalogue. A scoped rc client performs a notification round trip and
checks that UID 0 cannot publish system notifications or acquire the gated
endpoint without a grant. This is boot software authority, not user login
authorization. The probe also checks provider capability mode, root interference
denials, and process-local OOM exclusion.
A companion case authenticates a root image containing a tampered signed
provider; the manager must reject its catalogue before rc or READY.
General installed-world services and other writable data mounts
remain separate qualification work.
PID 1 delegates only console output before protected process creation.
The descriptor permits write and stat operations; input and terminal ioctls
are denied even through duplicates held by UID 0.
Protected rc reads from /dev/null and writes to this diagnostic stream.
The VM probe checks these restrictions and attempted rights widening.
This is not a trusted login display: diagnostic output is still shared.
Protected interactive maintenance requires a separate authenticated path.
Capsule prepares /Capabilities/Run as a private bounded tmpfs and retains its
kernel directory claim across manager lifetimes. SwitchBoard receives a scoped
descriptor grant, locks inheritance and transfer, and prepares runtime metadata.
Root descendants must fail namespace attacks and must not inherit the grant.
Capsule also prepares an empty /tmp as a 64 MiB sticky nosuid/noexec tmpfs.
Missing and nonempty mountpoints must be rejected before manager startup.
The guest checks root alias/replacement attacks, denied executable mappings,
and real shell, cat, cp, mv, rm, mkdir, mktemp and sort operations as UID 65534.
The result.json and programs.txt reports distinguish exercised operations,
READY-only services, test fixtures and programs without execution evidence.
Image inclusion alone is not a program compatibility pass.
Persistent /var/tmp, third-party package installation and user storage remain
unqualified. Package manifests are inventory inputs; production writable
exceptions require authenticated system policy, not a root-writable pkg database.
An additional signed-configuration case requests isolation off; the mandatory
kernel must keep enforcement enabled. File-handle APIs and NFS server
administration are restricted to bootstrap to prevent traversal bypasses.
PID 1 re-exec and RB_REROOT are denied after the first image starts, preserving
its confined claims until a new authenticated boot. The guest tests both paths
and retains ordinary child exec and EFI shutdown as positive controls.
The profile reserves device acquisition, mount changes and sysctl writes for
trusted bootstrap and is not yet suitable for a general installed world.
These tests cover firmware, loader, root-image authentication and this bootstrap floor; they do not
qualify complete runtime integrity, user authorization, recovery or rollback resistance.
.Ss Package manager qualification
The selected bootstrap package and its SHA256 remain explicit publication inputs.
After changing the package manager, rebuild and qualify that archive before
selecting it; update any generation database engine contract to match.
A previously selected archive is not replaced implicitly by a newer ports recipe.
.Ss Hardware firmware layout
Raw Bluetooth firmware in the custom hardware ports uses the base loader
locations under /usr/share/firmware. Rebuild base devd configuration and
hardware packages together when changing this layout. Locally modified devd
configuration needs explicit upgrade review; package configuration preservation
does not establish migration to the new defaults.
.Ss Package-derived generation inputs
Deploy inventories the exact archives fetched through its authenticated package
catalogue before creating a destination or boot environment.
It first snapshots the publication and explicit public keys into its private
run directory; inventory and installation use those same inputs.
This requires a trusted builder and does not provide a protected live updater.
Fetched packages must be reachable from the selected roots, with no missing
named dependencies, duplicate identities, origin substitutions or missing
advertised shared-library providers.
A dependency's recorded build version is not an exact version pin.
This closure check does not authorize a downgrade or replace generation approval.
It records package identities, archive and payload hashes, file types, numeric
and named ownership, modes, flags, timestamps, links and declared directories
in package-inventory.json beneath its retained deployment run directory.
It rejects duplicate payload paths, manifest/payload disagreement and conflicting
ownership or directory metadata.
Every directory declaration is validated against archive headers and other
packages, including declarations for an already known directory.
Supported declaration attributes are uname, gname, octal perm and native fflags;
unknown or malformed attributes fail.
Equivalent permission spellings agree by value; package order cannot resolve
conflicts.
Reconciliation requires complete directory ownership and permissions and
rejects independently asserted named and numeric owners as ambiguous.
Missing selected packages, missing dependencies and pkg fetch diagnostics are
fatal before destination creation.
Unsupported ACLs, extended attributes and special objects fail explicitly.
Configuration-file membership is recorded without granting a writable exception.
After generated replacements are composed, a declared root must be a directory
and no declared object may have a declared file or symlink as an ancestor.
Deployment rejects these conflicts before destination creation or target code
execution.
Valid leaf symlinks remain supported.
The composed hardlink graph must be acyclic and terminate at regular files.
A generated replacement cannot leave an existing hardlink targeting a symlink,
directory or missing object.
Boot-environment deployment derives private traversal directories from validated
package and generated objects, together with directories used by the installer.
Source mounts covering those directories are rejected.
Before running package scripts, the candidate must have no nested mounts;
a shared mount outside the payload inventory could still be reached by a script.
Existing traversal components must be physical directories on the candidate
filesystem.
This conservative staging restriction does not implement a production mutable
storage layout or establish resistance to a hostile builder.
Archive owner names must be resolved against the completed generation, not the host.
.Pp
This inventory is an input to the unfinished complete filesystem reconciliation
and authenticated BIP policy-generation path.
It does not authenticate an installed OS, apply runtime BIP classes, or qualify
normal startup, SSH or a protected boot-environment upgrade.
Repository verification and deployment explicitly set
.Va PKG_ENABLE_PLUGINS
to false for the selected pkg executable.
This is required even when an ambient configuration enables plugins.
Static pkg disables plugin loading independently; testing only that executable
cannot establish that the dynamic executable receives the correct option.
.Pp
Before destination creation, deploy requires every advertised shared-library
requirement to have a provider in the fetched package set.
The combined publication backend applies the same check before advancing latest.
Only selected package manifests supply providers; libraries on the builder do not.
This complements package-name dependency closure and does not prove ELF or dynamic
loading closure, compatible search paths, or runtime authorization.
.Pp
World package creation checks every staged plist against the package targets
selected by the current make configuration before creating or staging archives.
An old archive does not satisfy this check.
Kernel-owned and DTB payloads remain in their separate packaging paths; kernel
modules explicitly assigned to world packages enter the world metadata stream.
This coverage check does not establish payload authentication or runtime BIP.
.Pp
.Ss Package construction declarations
Deploy validates the optional 5bsd.construction JSON annotation in fetched
package manifests before creating its destination.
Format 1 describes named actions with explicit argv, regular-file inputs,
and finite regular-file outputs with numeric ownership, mode, flags and
explicit replacement intent.
Commands and inputs must bind to package payloads or declared input producers.
Conflicting outputs, dependency cycles, unsafe paths, foreign replacements,
and replacements with hardlink aliases fail.
The inventory records the declarations and payload hashes as declared-not-executed,
with outputs_approved false.
.Pp
Declarations neither execute code nor grant runtime authority, and they do not
replace the required generated-input manifest.
The construct action supplies bounded trusted-builder execution and measured
receipts; complete generator coverage, authenticated policy and generation
sealing remain required.
PAM, account databases, login classes and referenced security policy have the
same coverage requirements as executable payloads.
.Pp
World bootstrapping builds cap_mkdb with its matching text-only reader instead
of inheriting the host's compiled-database behavior.
Secure Boot qualification exercises the target generator and libc, including
stale input caches and oversized name rejection.
These generator checks do not supply complete construction receipts or qualify
a protected pkg upgrade.
The runtime package declares password and service database generation from
explicit master.passwd and services inputs.
It produces passwd and pwd.db with mode 0644, spwd.db with mode 0600, and
services.db with mode 0644.
These declarations cover fresh-generation derivation; account migrations,
protected credential writers, kernel hints and package
registration still require their own construction contracts.
.Pp
.Ss Collected boot transport check
The boot-check action checks collected image and boot archive bytes against their
common unsigned image receipt without extracting archive paths:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd boot-check --boot-image /usr/obj/collected/image.ufs \e
    --boot-archive /usr/obj/collected/boot.tar \e
    --image-receipt /usr/obj/collected/image-receipt.json
.Ed
It checks the complete boot namespace, contents, transport metadata, source and
read-back observation binding, hardlink consistency, and archive end records.
Unsupported extensions, duplicate receipt keys and invalid metadata reject.
Success does not authenticate the receipt, reconstruct filesystem metadata,
approve a release or establish that the system boots.
.Ss Boot transport staging
The boot-stage action accepts the same three inputs as boot-check, plus a new
.Fl -output
folder. It writes a makefs mtree manifest, private regular payload files and a
stage receipt containing the expected filesystem observations.
The finalized stage.json matches the returned report, including any startup
contract and stable generated-policy payload path. It is published by rename
only after staging completes; it is not a durable boot-selection journal.
Recorded modes,
ownership, flags and nanosecond timestamps enter the manifest; host ownership is
not used as image metadata. Archive symlinks are represented as manifest entries
and are never extracted on the host. Internal regular-file hardlinks are restored
in the payload store. Aliases outside /boot remain in the separate installed
root image; the outer transport contains only their /boot projection.
Hardlinked symlinks currently reject rather than losing their identity.
The outer filesystem root is a format directory (root:wheel, mode 0755, epoch
zero); it is not a reconstruction of the installed root directory.
The resulting makefs image still requires independent read-back against stage.json,
explicit boot policy and signing before boot qualification.
.Pp
The request summary labels the general signing option as
.Dq bundle-signing-mode .
In JSON request records,
.Dq signing_scope
is
.Dq system-bundles
and qualifies the
.Dq signing
field.
This mode does not describe generation-manifest or EFI signing, which use their
separate explicit key inputs. A request or plan is not evidence of completed
signing, firmware acceptance or root protection; inspect the operation receipts
and boot qualification results.
.Ss Generation filesystem image
The image action requires an alternate root, package inventory, final generated
manifest and a completed construction receipt:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd image --root /usr/obj/candidate \e
    --inventory /usr/obj/inputs/inventory.json \e
    --generated /usr/obj/constructed/generated.json \e
    --construction-receipt /usr/obj/constructed/construction.json \e
    --image-makefs /path/to/qualified/makefs \e
    --image-mdconfig /sbin/mdconfig --image-mount /sbin/mount \e
    --image-umount /sbin/umount --output /usr/obj/generation-image
.Ed
All four tools are explicit trusted-builder inputs and must reside outside the
candidate. Execution requires root for a newly allocated memory disk; use a
trusted disposable builder VM. The action re-reads package archives, checks the
construction receipt and reconciles the complete source filesystem. It builds
UFS2 without metadata overrides, checks that the source remains unchanged,
mounts the image read-only and compares all filesystem observations before
unmounting and detaching the memory disk. A failed check leaves diagnostic output
without a successful image receipt.
.Pp
With
.Fl -image-boot-archive ,
the action also writes boot.tar and boot.json for the complete verified /boot
namespace. It checks the source before and after export and verifies serialized
contents and transport metadata. The image receipt binds the archive digest and
complete observations, including flags and hardlink aliases outside /boot.
This is a transport artifact: boot-media construction must restore and reconcile
those observations. Ordinary tar extraction does not establish equivalent
metadata or authenticated boot media. This action takes no signing-key input.
.Pp
.Fl -image-verity-policy
selects an explicit JSON policy for the immutable base projection and requires
.Fl -storage-layout-policy .
The policy has exactly format (integer 1), block_size (a power of two from
512 through 65536), and salt_hex (zero through 256 bytes in lowercase hex).
The selected block size aligns the complete image using makefs size rounding;
it does not change the UFS sector size.
The builder emits system.verity (filesystem followed by hash tree) and
root.descriptor beside the base system.ufs, binds their hashes and policy in the
unsigned receipt, and checks retained bytes.
Retained validation recomputes every hash-tree block, compares the descriptor
root and checks the filesystem prefix against the selected source digest.
It uses block-bounded memory and reuses the data pass for the combined-file hash;
this is construction consistency, not release authentication.
Writable seed images retain their current format.
These outputs are not signed or enabled for boot by this option.
The optional kernel GEOM_VERITY configuration and mount.conf .verity directive
provide construction and final root-identity checks; they do not complete
authenticated staged BIP boot or runtime topology protection. The boot backend
requires explicit artifacts and a matching disk startup policy for staging;
this constructs a candidate and does not qualify protected boot.
Root-descriptor loading requires restricted compiled generation anchors; a loader
using general firmware manifest trust rejects it before reading descriptor bytes.
The existing secureboot-test generation-certificate input selects restricted
trust, but does not by itself qualify disk-backed boot.
The loader checks opened-device identity for selected-image ELF and raw-preload
inputs. The normal boot command checks the loaded root descriptor against the
retained selected descriptor before invoking the exec handler. Complete boot
provenance, release-key scope and handoff qualification remain required.
Compressed-loader source admission follows the retained backing handle to the
selected UFS image. Native gzip/bzip read, rewind and descriptor-lifetime tests
exist; compressed ELF execution and authenticated boot still require qualification.
Verified-root staging requires direct directories on the selected root for
/.mount and /dev; placement failures stop boot instead of using /mnt or
continuing with an incomplete mount topology.
Image construction and retained-layout verification require both slots to be
empty root-owned directories without group or other write permission in the
reconciled base inventory, with no writable-state boundary at or beneath them.
Supply these through package contents or declared generated-file policy; the
builder does not silently create them.
.Pp
The unsigned receipt records source and read-back observation digests, image
hash and size, tool hashes and input bindings. This action does not authenticate
inputs, sign a generation, verify boot, authorize activation or install an update.
Its plan performs no image construction or mounts.
.Ss Explicit package inventory
Create a construction inventory from an explicit selection of package archives:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd inventory --package-selection /usr/obj/selection.json \e
    --output /usr/obj/selected-inventory
.Ed
The selection is a JSON object with format set to 1 and a nonempty archives array.
Each entry has exactly path (an absolute archive filename) and sha256 (the
expected lowercase SHA-256 digest):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"format":1,"archives":[{"path":"/usr/obj/snapshot/example.pkg",
 "sha256":"<64 lowercase hexadecimal characters>"}]}
.Ed
The backend rereads payloads without extraction or script execution, checks each
expected hash, and requires declared dependencies and advertised shared-library
providers in the selected set.
Explicit conflicts between selected packages are rejected.
It writes inventory.json in a new output directory and binds the original
selection bytes by digest.
Existing output is never overwritten.
Format 1 requires the full selected dependency set.
Alternatively, format 2 selects roots from a quiescent repository directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"format":2,"repository":"/usr/obj/verified-snapshot",
 "roots":["5BSD-set-base","5BSD-kernel-generic-integrity"]}
.Ed
Select hardware and application package roots explicitly as needed.
The backend derives transitive dependencies from archive manifests, rejects
missing roots/dependencies and duplicate package names, then fully inventories
only the selected archives.
Selected manifests must remain unchanged between selection and full inventory.
Cycles are visited once.
The resulting inventory records the selected roots and measured archive hashes.
No host package database or hardcoded program inventory supplies the selection.
Neither format approves versions or authenticates the repository.
Format 2 does not replace a package solver for alternative/version constraints;
this repository must contain one already selected version per package name.
.Pp
Hashes bind bytes but do not authenticate a publisher.
Verify the repository with snapshot before selecting its archives.
Inventory does not apply BIP policy, construct a filesystem, sign a release or
authorize activation.
.Ss Trusted-builder construction
Construct executes the package-declared generators in a private alternate root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd construct --root /usr/obj/candidate \e
    --inventory /usr/obj/package-inventory.json \e
    --generated /usr/obj/initial-inputs.json \e
    --output /usr/obj/construction-result \e
    --construction-timeout 300
.Ed
The builder and its kernel must be trusted; execution requires a root shell
for temporary jail creation.
The command re-reads the referenced package archives and checks their hashes.
The selected set must include declared package dependencies with matching
origins and providers for advertised required shared libraries.
Dependency versions record build-time metadata; they are not exact solver pins.
These checks also apply when image creation revalidates construction inputs.
They do not authenticate the publisher, approve versions, prove runtime library
loading, or authenticate the initial input manifest.
The complete candidate must match these explicit expectations before execution.
Declared outputs cannot be preapproved by the initial manifest.
.Pp
Each generator runs in a temporary jail with a fixed environment and umask,
no IP networking, no mount permission, no child jails, and no supplied device
filesystem or inherited host directory descriptors.
The per-action timeout defaults to 300 seconds and permits 1 through 86400.
The builder removes the jail and waits for its descendants to exit before
measuring output; cleanup failure prevents acceptance.
.Pp
Only declared regular-file outputs may change.
The builder applies their declared ownership, mode and flags, and rejects
symlink or hardlink output aliases and unexplained filesystem changes.
Output-parent directory timestamps may change; the ZFS archive bookkeeping
flag is restored to its prior value.
Other metadata changes are errors.
The result includes construction.json with measured before/after hashes,
explicit inputs and output receipts, and generated.json for reconciliation.
These unsigned records do not prove all internal reads of an arbitrary program,
authenticate site policy, or authorize activation.
A failed candidate is retained for diagnosis and must not be deployed.
.Pp
This command is not yet a complete package installation transaction or a
root-resistant live updater.
It does not automatically replace deploy's package hooks.
Complete generator coverage, authenticated inputs and generation sealing,
normal startup, SSH, recovery and new-boot-environment qualification remain
required.
.Ss Package coverage review
Inventory-audit reviews an explicit package inventory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd inventory-audit --inventory /usr/obj/package-inventory.json \e
    --output /usr/obj/package-review
.Ed
The new output directory contains inventory-review.json, listing package objects,
executable candidates, state declarations and outstanding review gaps.
This report does not authenticate inputs, assign authority, discover every
runtime path or prove program compatibility.
Use generation reconciliation and functional qualification separately.
.Ss Offline generation reconciliation
Reconcile checks a quiescent alternate root against an explicit package
inventory and generated-input manifest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd reconcile --root /usr/obj/candidate \e
    --inventory /usr/obj/package-inventory.json \e
    --generated /usr/obj/generated-inputs.json \e
    --output /usr/obj/reconciliation-result
.Ed
The output directory must be new and outside the generation.
The command records reconciliation.json and exits unsuccessfully for unexplained
objects, missing objects or mismatched content, ownership, modes, flags
or links.
It never repairs the candidate or automatically accepts observed differences.
Unsupported special objects, ACLs, extended attributes and nested filesystems
are refused.
Archive ownership names resolve against declared, content-checked account files
inside the generation, without consulting the host account database.
Extraction preflight reads bounded, hash-checked account payloads from selected
packages and uses the same account parser before checking library ownership.
Unknown or ambiguous names are rejected; numeric archive ownership is not
substituted for a named candidate identity.
Compatibility aliases must have matching declared and observed lookup chains.
Their targets are resolved within the generation, opened without filesystem
symlink traversal, and content-checked again before deriving numeric ownership.
Configuration files receive the same checks as other package payloads.
.Pp
The generated-input JSON document has format 1 and an entries object keyed by
canonical absolute generation paths.
Each entry requires type, uid, gid, mode, flags_set, source and replaces.
Files additionally require size and sha256; symbolic links require target.
The source string identifies the declared generator or site input.
The replaces array must name exactly the SHA256 hashes of all package archives
owning a replaced path; new paths use an empty array.
Unknown fields and incomplete metadata are errors.
This declaration is not an attestation that a generator ran.
.Pp
Reconciliation does not authenticate its input files, classify storage or
writers, authorize symbolic-link targets, seal a generation, or verify BIP at
runtime.
The caller must supply trusted expectations and a quiescent staging filesystem;
this is not a verifier for a hostile live filesystem.
Directory timestamps are measured but not compared to package timestamps,
because installing children changes them.
Generated timestamps are also measured without a prescribed value.
All measured timestamps remain in the report for subsequent generation sealing.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--development-key file"
.It Fl -package-selection Ar file
Explicit archive bindings or repository package roots for inventory; see above.
.It Fl -inventory Ar file
Package payload inventory for inventory-audit, reconcile or construct.
.It Fl -generated Ar file
Explicit generated-input manifest, required for reconcile, construct and deploy.
Construct accepts initial inputs only; declared outputs must be produced by
execution.
Deploy derives its package inventory from authenticated downloads and does not
accept an external inventory.
.It Fl -storage-layout-policy Ar file
Optional explicit JSON storage partition policy for construct, reconcile or image.
Construct and reconcile also inspect declared protected consumers and record
ELF interpreter, library and search-path metadata bound to package and file
hashes.
Changed consumer bytes and malformed ELF are rejected; non-ELF consumers are
reported without claiming dependency coverage.
This metadata does not establish a resolved dependency set, runtime enforcement,
or authenticated input policy.
With construct, validate the completed filesystem after generated outputs and
the package database have been reconciled, before reporting success.
Retain storage-layout.json and bind its digest, the policy digest and final
filesystem digest in the construction receipt.
A planning invocation cannot validate this final state.
Format 1 has a boundaries array of objects containing path and content,
where content is ordinary or protected.
Format 2 additionally requires a protected_consumers array.
Format 3 also requires an explicit target_elf object with elf_class (32 or 64),
byte_order (little or big), machine (ELF machine number) and page_size.
A format-3 consumer may specify code_loading equal to bip.
For that consumer, reconciliation additionally requires matching native ELF
identification and exactly one ABI note and restrictive feature-control note
within the target note bounds.
This checks declared image metadata, not whether the configured manager was
completely enumerated or whether the selected kernel and interpreter implement
the required runtime contract.
Each entry supplies consumer, package_archive_sha256 and a nonempty inputs array.
The consumer must be a direct regular-file payload from that exact package
archive, with unchanged bytes in the completed generation.
Each input supplies path and presence, either required or optional.
The consumer and every input lookup must remain in immutable storage, including
intermediate aliases; protected mutable storage is not accepted for these inputs.
Optional inputs may be absent only at a final leaf with immutable parents.
Alias lookup observes the target kernel symlink traversal limit.
Exceeding that limit rejects required and optional protected inputs alike.
The report records input hashes and lookup observations without claiming
complete dependency coverage, authenticated approval or runtime enforcement.
This extension preserves the existing configuration and executable restrictions.
Boundaries must be disjoint existing physical directories.
All objects outside them remain in the immutable partition.
The check requires exact filesystem reconciliation, retains every seed object's
metadata, rejects crossing hardlinks, and requires declared state to be covered.
Package state-requirements format 2 permits an optional directory_mapping
for directory requirements. The path field remains the fallback.
The checker selects a mapping only when the completed filesystem contains it;
a present mapping must resolve to a directory. Dangling links, cycles and
non-directory targets fail. Mapping parents must be physical directories.
Format 1 does not accept the new field.
Reports retain the declared fallback, mapping, selected path and resolved path.
The selected path still needs an explicit writable boundary; the declaration
neither classifies content nor grants authority.
Installation policy alias operations may create the package-declared mapping
at an absent, unowned destination using the existing validation rules.
Ordinary writable storage may contain executable files and package configuration,
including ordinary configuration aliases.
Package configuration membership alone does not require immutable storage.
Explicitly declared protected consumers and their inputs must remain immutable
throughout lookup, including intermediate directories and aliases.
Protected state requirements likewise cannot traverse ordinary storage.
An optional directory mapping must have a safe lookup path even when absent,
so creating it later cannot redirect protected state through ordinary storage.
The report does not install seeds, construct attachments, authenticate policy
or authorize runtime access.
This is a construction check for the selected storage layout, including ordinary
application storage; it does not implement live package transactions or assign
trust to applications installed there.
With image, construct an immutable base image and separate seed images using
complete mtree inputs, then mount each read-only and compare all observations.
The complete source is checked before and after the image set; each image
retains its independent filesystem readback. Successful image receipts are
written only after the final source check. Progress diagnostics go to stderr.
Seed roots retain their original metadata; empty base attachment slots have
UID/GID zero, mode 0755 and no flags, with the original timestamps.
The aggregate receipt binds policy, projections and individual image receipts.
Before reporting success, independently re-read the retained aggregate and child
receipts and verify every image and mtree digest against the selected inputs.
Write verification.json with the explicit scope retained-storage-image-consistency.
This is transport consistency, not authentication or a second filesystem readback.
These images are unsigned construction outputs; seed installation, attachment
activation are not implemented.
With image-boot-archive, export the unchanged immutable /boot namespace under
base/boot.tar and bind it to base/receipt.json.
Boot-check and boot-stage accept this immutable base receipt; mutable seed
receipts cannot be used as boot roots.
The aggregate image set and seed images must be retained for installation.
.It Fl -installation-policy Ar file
Optional explicit JSON installation configuration for construct only.
Format 1 retains replacement-only behavior.
Format 2 requires an operation field on every entry: create or replace.
Format 3 also accepts alias entries, described below.
Format 4 adds final-phase relocate entries.
Format 5 additionally accepts replace-template with the same source, hash,
metadata and exact owning archive fields as replace.
The destination must be declared by its owning package in the
5bsd.installation-templates annotation, a JSON string with format 1 and a paths
array of canonical absolute filenames.
Each declared template must be a nonexecutable regular package payload,
without file flags, and must not be a pkg configuration file.
Debug and manual subpackages must not inherit declarations for unowned files.
This permits installation-specific immutable policy without making it mutable
package configuration or authorizing runtime writes.
All existing source hashing, parent, hardlink, generated-output conflict and
root-builder checks still apply.
The entries object maps canonical candidate paths to source, sha256, size,
uid, gid, mode and replaces fields.
Source names an explicit canonical regular file outside the candidate.
Replaces lists the exact owning package archive SHA256.
The constructor checks these bindings and source bytes against the declared
package object graph before extraction.
The construction VM runner performs the same check before launching a guest.
These checks do not attest a completed filesystem; installation still reconciles
the real candidate and rechecks source bytes.
A new package archive needs a new explicit binding even when its configuration
payload is unchanged.
Before updating a binding, compare the owning package, file content and security
metadata with the previously selected input; changed templates require review
and recomposition of the intended configuration.
Destinations must be unaliased regular package-declared configuration files,
with existing declared directory ancestors and no file flags.
For format-2 create entries, the destination must instead be absent from the
observed tree and package payloads, and replaces must be an empty array.
Parent directories must already be declared; creation never makes ancestors.
Neither operation may overlap generated inputs or declared generator outputs.
The constructor checks module and library plans for destination conflicts
before installing configuration.
Configuration metadata must be root-owned, nonexecutable and not writable by
group or others. File flags are zero.
The trusted root builder stages and verifies all input bytes before
installing files, reconciles the complete tree, and binds the policy and
configuration metadata into its receipt and generated manifest.
Original package database configuration baselines remain unchanged.
For a format-3 alias entry, the fields are operation (alias), target,
package and requirement. The destination must match exactly one named state
requirement of the selected package and must be absent from the candidate,
package inventory and generated outputs. Its parents must already be directories.
Target must be a canonical absolute candidate path, other than root or the
destination itself. Candidate-only resolution rejects cycles and traversal
through nondirectories; an existing target must match the requirement type.
A missing target is permitted for state created at boot.
The generated link is root-owned, mode 0777, with zero file flags.
Aliases do not provision storage, authorize writers or approve runtime mounts.
For a format-4 relocate entry, the fields are operation (relocate), target,
package and requirement. The source path must match one package-declared file
state requirement. After all generators and database construction finish, the
source must be a regular nonexecutable file without flags or other hardlinks.
The target must be absent and unowned; both paths need physical existing parents.
Targets cannot collide with other installation entries or relocation targets.
The constructor moves the file preserving its bytes and metadata, replaces its
original path with an explicitly initialized root-owned alias, syncs the affected
file and directories, then reconciles the entire candidate again.
The receipt records the selected policy, original observations, moves and generated
records. Storage classification and writable-boundary validation still apply.
The retained verifier reconstructs the prior manifest from package and generation
inputs, reverses the declared moves in its filesystem observation, and checks
predecessor continuity, original content and metadata, exact generated records,
and the resulting filesystem digest. Parent observations permit only directory
timestamp changes. This verifies consistency, not policy approval or boot trust.
A failed construction must be discarded; no successful generated manifest
is emitted. This option neither approves policy nor grants runtime writers.
.It Fl -library-policy Ar file
Optional explicit format-1 JSON library-search policy for construct only.
Fields are format, program, directories and output.
Select a packaged strict-mode ldconfig executable, an ordered list of canonical
absolute directories and a new hints output path.
Directories and ancestors must be verified package-declared directories owned
by root without group or other write permission.
The constructor invokes ldconfig with -S, binds directory metadata and verifies
the exact ELF hints contents before accepting a receipt.
Outputs are root:wheel mode 0444.
The shipped library-policy.json example selects /lib and /usr/lib and generates
/boot/ld-elf.so.hints for protected BIP execution. Pass it explicitly with this
option when constructing that system profile. Approved additional search
directories are policy inputs, not discovered from a running host or mutable
ports cache. Ordinary execution retains its separate /var/run hints cache.
This trusted-builder input does not grant runtime storage authority or
authenticate packages and policy.
.It Fl -module-policy Ar file
Optional explicit format-1 JSON module policy for construct only.
Selects a packaged generator, module directories, package and basename patterns,
required or optional selections, and fixed output metadata.
An example is installed in
.Pa /usr/share/examples/bsd-build/module-policy.json .
Only matching package payloads become generator inputs.
Existing output requires explicit replacement intent and must match its owning
package payload; an unexplained file cannot be adopted.
The constructor records input selection, policy and output hashes and verifies
actual jailed execution before accepting generated state.
The policy is bounded to 64 groups and 1024 selected inputs, with command size
limits enforced before execution.
Supplying this input does not authenticate it or authorize module loading.
.It Fl -certificate-policy Ar file
Optional explicit certificate policy for construct only.
The format-1 JSON policy selects package payloads by package name and path
prefix,
additional verified files, explicit role overrides, a packaged generator, and
output paths and metadata.
An example is installed in
.Pa /usr/share/examples/bsd-build/certificate-policy.json .
The constructor derives a bounded output plan in a temporary jail, requires
planning to leave the candidate unchanged, and checks actual outputs against
that plan before recording a receipt.
Output directories must already exist and be empty; the bundle must be absent.
Supplying this input does not authenticate it or authorize a trust change.
.It Fl -extract-tar Ar executable
For construct, materialize the selected package payloads into an empty root
before running the declared generators.
The generated-input manifest must declare explicit root directory metadata;
see root-layout.json. It may also declare otherwise unowned directories with
explicit metadata, provenance and empty replacement lists.
Package-owned objects cannot be replaced, and files or symlinks cannot be added
through this extraction layout. All parents must be declared directories.
Directory declarations do not grant runtime write access or select writable mounts.
The root must have a root-owned mode-0700 parent in the trusted builder.
Archive hashes are checked before and after extraction.
The selected tar ignores account names during extraction; final ownership is
resolved from the verified candidate account files, followed by full filesystem
reconciliation.
Existing roots containing objects are refused.
Failure may retain a partial private tree, which must not be used as a generation.
This option does not authenticate inputs or authorize installation or activation.
.It Fl -database-pkg Ar executable
Explicit static pkg executable for optional fresh database construction.
Construct requires all five database options together.
The executable hash must match the format-3 database contract.
.It Fl -database-sqlite Ar executable
Explicit SQLite executable used for read-only verification of the result.
.It Fl -database-contract Ar file
Format-3 contract selecting engine/schema hashes, installation epoch and
package identity/control policy.
The reviewed profile requires schema 1000 and populated archive solver digests.
Selected package versions must exactly match this contract.
Recorded dependency build versions are not exact version pins; dependency
origins and the complete selected archive set remain checked.
.It Fl -database-policy Ar file
Explicit output pathname, uid, gid, mode and flags_set for the generated database.
The destination must be absent beneath an already verified directory.
An example is installed in
.Pa /usr/share/examples/bsd-build/database-output-policy.json .
.It Fl -database-osversion Ar integer
Positive 32-bit OSVERSION of the selected target build.
Database construction uses a separate restricted jail, with local-only archive
registration and scripts, triggers, plugins and repository updates disabled.
After verification, the database is installed without following symlinks or
replacing existing files, and is included in full filesystem reconciliation
and generated-file receipts.
These inputs and receipts are not authenticated by this offline action.
.It Fl -construction-timeout Ar seconds
Per-generator execution timeout for construct, from 1 through 86400 seconds.
The default is 300 seconds.
.It Fl -root Ar directory
Existing alternate root for verify, reconcile or construct, or a new
destination directory for deploy.
Deploy refuses an existing directory.
For deploy, its parent must be root-owned with mode 0700.
This keeps the candidate inaccessible to other local users even when package
extraction restores public modes on directories inside it.
Boot-environment deployment uses the private deployment work directory.
Secureboot-test requires an existing staged root with the selected built kernel.
.It Fl -qemu Ar file
QEMU executable for secureboot-test; default /usr/local/bin/qemu-system-x86_64.
.It Fl -qemu-data Ar directory
Firmware directory for secureboot-test; default /usr/local/share/qemu.
.It Fl -uefi-vars-tool Ar file
virt-fw-vars executable for secureboot-test; default /usr/local/bin/virt-fw-vars.
.It Fl -test-generation-key Ar path
Host-only disposable generation signing key for secureboot-test.
Requires
.Fl -test-generation-certificate
and one explicit test case independent of secondary firmware db trust.
The second-db-cert, second-dbx-cert and second-dbx-hash cases are rejected
in this mode; they test firmware-backed manifest trust.
Do not supply production signing keys to disposable qualification runs.
The firmware-signed-manifest case signs the manifest with the disposable EFI
signer and requires rejection as an untrusted manifest signer.
The revoked-generation-manifest case adds the generation certificate to dbx
while leaving the EFI signer trusted, and requires certificate revocation rejection.
Both require this explicit generation identity and case-specific loader evidence.
A generic loader failure does not satisfy either case.
.It Fl -test-generation-certificate Ar path
Public certificate for the generation test key.
The frontend rebuilds the test loader with this TRUST_ANCHORS input and
WITHOUT_LOADER_EFI_TRUST_DB=yes, and supplies the pair to the runner.
The path must contain only letters, digits, slash, dot, underscore or dash
because it is passed through the make trust-anchor list.
Firmware enrollment and EFI signing still use runner-generated disposable keys.
A planned or compiled test is not proof of firmware enforcement.
.It Fl -test-case Ar name
Select one explicit secureboot-test runner case, such as
.Dq unsigned-loader .
The runner validates the case name and its required inputs.
Without this option, the full default suite and complete-suite evidence are required.
With it, the independent verifier checks the selected case; this is partial
qualification and must not be reported as a complete-suite pass.
No host installation or reboot is performed.
.It Fl -public-key Ar file
Trusted code-signing public key for verify, deploy, bsdinstall, or media.
.It Fl -repository Ar directory
Completed combined generation under /usr/obj/usr/src/repo for deploy,
bsdinstall, or media.
A latest symlink is resolved once to select a specific generation.
.It Fl -pkg-public-key Ar file
Trusted package repository public key for deploy, bsdinstall, or media.
Public keys copied for catalogue verification, deployment, installer preparation
or media must contain one PUBLIC KEY or RSA PUBLIC KEY PEM block only.
They are parsed and emitted as canonical public-key bytes; combined public/private
PEM files, extra objects and malformed keys are rejected before copying.
.It Fl -bootenv Ar name
Create a new recursive boot-environment clone for deploy instead of --root.
The clone remains inactive.
Existing names are not reused or destroyed.
.It Fl -clone-from Ar BE|BE@snapshot
Select the source for a new boot environment.
Defaults to the active boot
environment.
A fully qualified dataset beneath the selected boot-environment
root is also accepted.
Only the new clone is made writable.
The helper checks the source dataset GUID before creation and again before
making the candidate writable.
An explicit source snapshot also has its GUID recorded and rechecked.
Dataset identity replies must contain unique expected fields and a valid
nonzero 64-bit GUID; malformed replies stop deployment.
The candidate dataset GUID and clone origin are retained and checked before
making it writable, before and after mounting, and before temporary devfs
and boot-environment cleanup unmount.
Temporary devfs mount identity and topology are recorded and rechecked separately.
If identity cannot be established or changes, cleanup retains the mounts for
operator diagnosis.
An observed replacement aborts without automatic deletion or unmount by name.
These checks do not serialize concurrent ZFS namespace changes.
An observed identity change aborts deployment and preserves any candidate.
These consistency checks do not serialize concurrent ZFS administration.
.It Fl -be-root Ar dataset
Select an explicit ZFS boot-environment parent dataset, for example
zroot/ROOT.
Requires both --bootenv and --clone-from.
This also permits creating a candidate in a separate test pool.
.It Fl -config Ar file
Read a literal key=value configuration file.
Command-line options override
file settings regardless of their position.
Unknown and duplicate keys fail.
Blank lines and whole-line comments beginning with # are accepted.
Values are literal: do not add shell quotes, expansions, or inline comments.
The file is never sourced as shell code.
.It Fl -arch Ar native|amd64|arm64|riscv64
Target architecture; native defaults to the current machine.
The make pairs are amd64/amd64, arm64/aarch64, and riscv/riscv64.
Host signing tools remain native to the build host.
.It Fl -jobs Ar count|auto
Parallel compilation jobs.
The default auto uses all logical CPUs.
.It Fl -src-conf Ar file
World option configuration passed as SRCCONF; defaults to /etc/src.conf.
Use /dev/null to disable that configuration file.
.It Fl -make-conf Ar file
Make configuration passed as __MAKE_CONF; defaults to /etc/make.conf.
These two files are trusted make input, unlike the literal frontend config.
.It Fl -kernel-dir Ar directory
Directory containing the selected kernel configuration, passed as KERNCONFDIR.
.It Fl -pkg-key Ar file
Existing unencrypted RSA private key for both base and combined repository
catalogues.
It must be private and outside the publication repository.
This is independent of the Ed25519 executable-signing key.
.It Fl -candidate-publication
Create a complete package generation for explicit validation without advancing
latest.
Applies only to build/packages and preserves the standard repository location.
Package, dependency, signature and kernel/hardware checks still apply.
The completed generation can be selected explicitly for VM installation;
it does not constitute VM qualification or a protected production release.
The normal publication path still advances latest only after successful checks.
.It Fl -boot-media-makefs Ar file
Optional explicit makefs tool for boot-stage media construction. Requires EFI
signing and all four remaining media options below. The complete mtree builds a
UFS2 partition; the signed EFI artifact builds a FAT16 ESP. mkimg combines them
into media/disk.img. Tools and staged inputs are hashed before and after use.
Receipt success denotes construction only; readback and firmware/normal startup
qualification remain separate requirements.
.It Fl -boot-media-mkimg Ar file
Explicit mkimg executable for GPT disk assembly.
.It Fl -boot-efi-fallback Ar name
EFI fallback filename for the selected architecture, such as BOOTX64.EFI.
Only the BOOT<uppercase letters or digits>.EFI filename form is accepted.
The name must match the signed loader's PE machine and optional-header format;
staging rejects mismatches before media output is created. Supported machine
formats are IA32, X64, AArch64 and RISC-V64 EFI applications. Header recognition
does not qualify firmware or runtime support for those architectures.
.It Fl -boot-esp-mib Ar size
ESP capacity in MiB, from 16 through 1024. Insufficient capacity fails construction.
.It Fl -boot-free-percent Ar percent
Additional free space for the UFS2 boot partition, from 0 through 50 percent.
.It Fl -boot-efi-path Ar path
Optional logical /boot path of a regular inventory file to sign as an EFI loader.
Requires manifest signing and boot-efi-signer. Staging checks its size and digest,
keeps an unsigned copy, and emits efi/loader.efi with input/output/tool hashes.
Staging requires independent Authenticode verification under the selected
signing certificate before media creation. It does not establish firmware
acceptance, enrolled trust or generation bootability.
.It Fl -boot-efi-signer Ar file
Explicit uefisign executable. It receives the key path, not a key descriptor,
preserving its parent/sandboxed-parser key separation. Staging checks key identity
and metadata and the signer hash before and after signing.
.It Fl -boot-efi-signature-verifier Ar file
Required explicit osslsigncode executable supporting require-leaf-hash.
Staging verifies the image digest and signature with the selected public
certificate bundle and pins the first certificate as the required signer.
Timestamp trust and CRL checks are disabled for this offline artifact check;
firmware trust and revocation require separate qualification.
The receipt records the verifier, OpenSSL, certificate and signed-image hashes.
A failed check prevents media assembly and successful stage publication.
.It Fl -boot-efi-signing-key Ar file
Optional private key for EFI executable signing, separate from manifest signing.
Requires boot-stage EFI inputs and boot-efi-signing-certificate.
If neither separate EFI credential option is supplied, EFI signing continues to
use the manifest credentials.
The receipt records explicit-efi or manifest-signing selection and the EFI
certificate digest; choosing separate inputs does not prove enrolled trust.
.It Fl -boot-efi-signing-certificate Ar file
Public certificate-only PEM for the separate EFI signing key.
Both separate credential options must be supplied together.
The backend rejects mixed or private PEM before copying a certificate snapshot
into efi/certificate.pem, then supplies that snapshot to the signer.
The snapshot digest is checked after signing.
Cryptographic signature verification is required during staging; firmware
verification remains a separate qualification step.
.It Fl -boot-signing-key Ar file
Boot manifest paths are resolved as complete logical pathnames, counting
directory aliases against the same symlink traversal limit as file aliases.
Unresolvable paths are rejected before manifest signing.
.Pp
Optional private manifest signing key for boot-stage. Requires boot-generated,
boot-signing-certificate and boot-openssl. The generated manifest is signed,
verified against the certificate public key, and included with its detached
signature and certificate in makefs inputs. No private key is retained in the
stage. The original key is opened without following a symlink and supplied only
to the signing subprocess through standard input; no temporary key copy is made.
Boot inputs must not occupy /boot/manifest, /boot/manifest.sig or
/boot/manifest.certs as files, directories or aliases. These output-name
collisions are rejected before creating the stage or invoking the signer.
Signature correctness does not establish release authorization, enrolled
firmware trust, filesystem readback or boot qualification.
.It Fl -boot-signing-certificate Ar file
Certificate-only PEM chain whose first certificate corresponds to the explicit
manifest signing key. Combined key/certificate files and non-certificate material
are rejected before staging. Every certificate is parsed and canonical public PEM
is emitted; both input and emitted-certificate digests are recorded.
This parsing does not validate chain trust or release authorization.
.It Fl -boot-openssl Ar file
Explicit absolute OpenSSL executable for manifest signing and verification.
Its digest is checked before and after the operation and recorded in the receipt.
.It Fl -boot-root-path Ar path
Logical path in the boot inventory of the root image supplied by boot-image.
Required for boot media construction. Its size and digest must match the
verified image receipt before staging or signing begins. The selected path is
recorded in the stage receipt; no filename is assumed.
The receipt reread used for staging must match the digest returned by input
verification before output is created; replacing it between verification and
staging is rejected.
The archive copy pass also checks entry order and completeness against that
receipt before writing the filesystem manifest or signing generated inputs.
These consistency checks do not authenticate the receipt's author.
This binds the image bytes, but does not validate loader configuration semantics
or authorize the generation.
.It Fl -boot-verity-image Ar file
Explicit combined filesystem/hash-tree image for boot-check or boot-stage.
Requires --boot-verity-descriptor and a matching immutable-base image receipt.
The checker validates the boot archive, original filesystem digest, complete
retained tree and descriptor together. Results establish unsigned consistency,
not signer authentication, firmware boot or activation authorization.
.It Fl -boot-verity-descriptor Ar file
Explicit root descriptor paired with --boot-verity-image.
Both inputs must be regular nonsymlink files. The descriptor is read into one
bounded private buffer. Disk staging additionally requires an explicit verity
boot policy. It copies the checked artifacts at that policy's paths and includes
them with the generated contract in the staged signing inventory. Kernel
disk-root admission and boot qualification remain incomplete.
.It Fl -boot-policy Ar file
JSON startup contract for boot-check or boot-stage, required when signing a
staged manifest. Preload mode is {"version":1,"mode":"preloaded"}.
The backend renders /boot/boot-policy.lua, binds its bytes through generated-file
staging, and includes it in the loader manifest when signing is requested.
A conflicting generated or packaged object is refused.
.Pp
With both verity artifact inputs, boot-check and boot-stage accept mode="verity"
and the fields image, descriptor, cache_blocks and heap_bytes. The two paths
must be distinct canonical /boot paths containing only ASCII letters, digits,
underscore, slash, dot, plus or hyphen. Cache blocks must be a nonnegative integer;
heap bytes must be positive; both are bounded by 2^63-1. The renderer derives
descriptor_sha256 from checked artifacts, never a caller override, and represents
memory bounds as exact decimal strings for the loader.
.Pp
The offline result contains unsigned startup-contract text and its digest.
transport_paths_bound and startup_integration_verified remain false: artifact
consistency does not bind logical paths in completed boot media or qualify boot.
Staging derives the root-image binding from the combined verified image, rejects
conflicting generated/packaged/signing paths and requires any explicit root path
to match the policy. Its result sets staged_paths_bound=true, while
transport_paths_bound and startup_integration_verified remain false until actual
media readback and boot qualification. Required Secure Boot startup validates the
contract before loading configuration or executables; disk mode commits required
selection, checks the descriptor digest and publishes the retained descriptor
before boot. Kernel disk-root admission remains incomplete.
.It Fl -boot-generated Ar file
Optional JSON object of generated boot file bindings for boot-stage.
Each absolute /boot path maps to source (absolute regular input path) and
observation (type=file, uid, gid, mode, flags_set, mtime, mtime_nsec, size,
sha256). Parents must exist in the package-derived boot inventory; replacement
of packaged objects is refused. Bytes are copied and checked before recording
success. The completed inventory produces loader.manifest.unsigned alongside
the makefs inputs. Signing, filesystem readback and boot qualification remain
separate requirements.
.It Fl -package-tool Ar file
Explicit host pkg executable used to create packages and verify/publish the
combined repository, passed as PKG_CMD for build/packages.
Requires an absolute path to a readable regular executable, without shell or
make metacharacters or whitespace. The normal backend default applies if omitted.
This selects a trusted build tool; it does not authenticate the tool or replace
the separate target bootstrap archive and its expected digest.
.It Fl -application-selection Ar file
Explicit format-1 package archive selection for build/packages publication.
The JSON object contains format=1 and an archives array of absolute path and
SHA256 bindings, using the maintained package-selection schema.
Ordinary application names, versions and archive bytes are preserved.
The publisher validates them against the combined base/hardware selection before
catalogue signing; this input does not authorize replacing system packages.
The selection path must be a regular file without whitespace or make/shell
metacharacters. Configuration files use application_selection.
These are explicit trusted-builder inputs: hashes bind bytes but do not establish
repository authentication. Fetching/resolving remote application repositories
remains a separate step. This option neither installs packages nor activates an
upgrade. Repository-root (format-2) selections are not accepted by this interface.
.It Fl -pkg-package Ar file
Explicit target-compatible pkg bootstrap archive to include in the combined
publication before catalogue signing.
Applies to build/packages and requires
.Fl -pkg-package-sha256 .
The archive must name pkg, match the target ABI, and declare an OS version
no newer than the target.
A base repository already containing pkg rejects a second bootstrap input.
This supplies offline installation without fetching a package manager separately.
The archive path must be a readable regular file without whitespace or make/shell
metacharacters. Configuration files use pkg_package and pkg_package_sha256.
.It Fl -pkg-package-sha256 Ar digest
Expected SHA256 of the explicit bootstrap archive.
The publisher checks the copied bytes before reading its manifest and includes
those bytes in the signed catalogue.
The digest pins the publisher's selected input; upstream provenance remains the
publisher's responsibility.
.It Fl -hardware-profile Ar file
Hardware port selection for build/packages, passed as HARDWARE_PORT_LIST.
Defaults to the maintained hardware-ports file for the target architecture.
The profile and ports checkout are checked before compilation.
.It Fl -distfiles Ar directory
Absolute ports download-cache directory for build/packages.
Defaults to distfiles beneath the selected ports checkout.
.It Fl -board Ar generic|rpi4|rpi5|rpi-zero2w
Board build defaults; generic leaves architecture and kernel unchanged.
Raspberry Pi profiles select arm64; Pi 4 and Zero 2 W default to GENERIC-RPI,
while Pi 5 selects GENERIC for an experimental UEFI path.
An explicit kernel overrides the default.
This selects compilation inputs only: board firmware assembly and signed disk
image integration are not yet exposed by this frontend.
Non-generic boards are therefore rejected by media.
These profiles do not establish hardware qualification or Pi 5 device support.
.It Fl -kernel Ar name
Kernel configuration; defaults to KERNCONF or GENERIC.
.It Fl -production
Build with WITH_MALLOC_PRODUCTION=yes.
Alias for --build-profile production.
Build and packages default to production; stage defaults to development.
.It Fl -build-profile Ar production|development|debug
Production enables production malloc.
Development explicitly enables debug malloc.
Debug also supplies DEBUG_FLAGS=-g for compiler debug information.
It does not disable optimization or select a different kernel configuration;
use --kernel and the configuration files for kernel diagnostics.
Build profiles are independent of executable-signing modes.
.It Fl -allow-debug-publication
Permit development/debug package publication for an explicit test only,
passing ALLOW_DEBUG_MALLOC=yes to maintained publication checks.
Rejected for production profiles and non-package actions.
Do not distribute these test generations as production releases.
.It Fl -incremental
Use WITHOUT_CLEAN=yes instead of the default clean build.
.It Fl -media-format Ar all|iso|memstick
Media artifacts to create; all (the default) creates both disc1.iso and
memstick.img.
Architecture selection applies to the existing target world,
kernel, and publication; media does not rebuild them.
.It Fl -output Ar directory
New output directory under /usr/obj for stage or media.
Defaults are unique directories under /usr/obj/usr/src/stages or
/usr/obj/usr/src/media, respectively.
.It Fl -signing Ar mode
Unsigned, development, or signed.
With no explicit mode, supplying a key selects signed; otherwise unsigned.
Unsigned needs no signing tools and retains development manifests.
Development creates and retains a local Ed25519 key on demand.
Signed requires an existing key and never generates a replacement identity.
.It Fl -key Ar file
Private signing key for signed mode.
Only its public key enters the image.
.It Fl -development-key Ar file
Persistent development key location, outside the stage and repository.
The default is /usr/obj/usr/src/signing-keys/development.pem.
.It Fl -relaxed-signatures
Sign the stage but omit the bundle signature-enforcement marker for testing.
This does not disable rejection of recognized invalid executable signatures.
.It Fl -build-backend Ar bsd-make
Select the build adapter.
Currently only bsd-make is implemented; unknown
backends are rejected.
.It Fl -format Ar text|json
Output format for plan.
JSON emits one request record followed by one record
per step, including its argument vector.
.It Fl -publish-repository Ar directory
Final combined publication destination; defaults to /usr/obj/usr/src/repo.
Base and hardware build staging remain under /usr/obj.
.It Fl -ports Ar directory
Custom ports checkout for packages.
Defaults to PORTSDIR or /usr/obj/5BSD-ports.
.El
.Sh DEPLOYMENT
Deploy verifies the signed repository and fetches authenticated packages
before
creating a target.
It installs base, the selected kernel and matching hardware
using pkg inside the target chroot, then verifies package checksums and signed
system bundles with native capsign.
Deploy requires --generated with the explicit generated-input manifest described
under Offline generation reconciliation.
It snapshots this input with the publication and validates its metadata and exact
package replacement declarations before creating the target.
After removing temporary installation inputs and unmounting devfs, it reconciles
every candidate object against the fetched package inventory and that manifest.
Reconciliation occurs while an inactive boot environment is still mounted.
The deployment report records the candidate dataset, GUID and origin, checked
before and after reconciliation, or the destination path for a directory target.
Observed identity replacement aborts before writing this report.
These checks do not serialize other ZFS writers or authorize activation.
A PASS reconciliation report does not establish successful unmount or boot.
Unexplained objects, modified payloads or mismatched metadata fail deployment;
the candidate and reconciliation.json remain available for diagnosis.
No scan automatically generates accepted exceptions, including for configuration
or the package database.
Private signing keys are not copied into the target.
Deploy is trusted-builder preparation, not a live BIP updater.
The current delegated BIP management interface excludes clone/receive and pool
operations; it does not authorize the conventional bectl path used here.
Authenticated activation, rollback selection and retirement require a separate
protected update implementation and qualification.
For boot-environment deployment, package state and system software must be
private to the root clone.
Separately mounted or symlinked etc, usr/local,
var/db/pkg, and Capabilities/System paths are rejected pending an explicit
migration design.
Shared home/data storage is not migrated.
The new dataset
identity, snapshot origin, and writable mount are checked before installation.
Root privileges and a native-architecture host are required.
Package scripts run inside the chroot using the host kernel.
Work logs and failed destinations are retained.
An unmount failure never causes
recursive cleanup of a mounted boot environment.
The command reports scope packages-bundles-and-filesystem only after successful
package and bundle verification, filesystem reconciliation, and unmount.
The report records the input hashes and package authentication method.
The deployment backend loads hardware validation and stage verification from
its own source tree, keeping validation helpers consistent with that backend.
A stage-verification failure retains the candidate for diagnosis and prevents
a successful deployment report.
The generated manifest remains a trusted builder input; this workflow does not
verify generator execution provenance, authenticate the completed generation,
apply BIP writer policy or provide a root-resistant live updater.
This path requires signed package and executable inputs; unsigned deployment
support and full VM deployment validation remain outstanding.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The sample file is /usr/share/examples/bsd-build/bsd-build.conf.sample.
Every long option except config has a corresponding configuration key;
replace hyphens with underscores (for example clone_from or pkg_public_key).
Boolean values are yes or no.
Options remain subject to action-specific
validation even when supplied by the configuration file.
Boolean values are yes or no.
Signing values match --signing.
No file is loaded implicitly.
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
Signing tools are compiled natively in the run directory before signing work;
target binaries are never used as host tools.
A native C compiler and host crypto, UCL and MD libraries are required.
The source tree contains the maintained host-tool recipe and stage helpers.
Executable signing is separate from repository signing and rollback policy.
Private keys must remain outside staged images and published repositories.
Fresh signing stages must have an empty code trust catalogue.
Stage verification requires exactly the requested code key in that catalogue;
missing, substituted and additional entries are rejected.
This workflow currently supports one system code signer per stage.
Protecting release keys from compromised build-host root requires external
key custody and an independently authorized signing process.
Before building, the frontend checks existing key permissions and resolves
existing ancestors of future key and staging paths without creating them.
The stage signing helper repeats the key checks when it uses the key.
.Pp
OS permissions must permit writing the object root and stage.
The tool reports permission failures instead of relocating output to a home
directory.
Inherited make output-directory overrides are cleared.
.Pp
Complete hardware publication currently requires a native target and an
installed hardware profile.
Foreign targets are rejected before compilation; stage remains available
for cross-compiling a private world/kernel image.
.Sh MACHINE-READABLE PLANS
The plan-only option
.Fl -format Cm json
emits newline-delimited JSON records with schema org.5bsd.build-plan.v1.
The first record describes the selected request and backend identifiers.
The verification_scope field is packages-bundles-and-filesystem for deploy,
offline-filesystem-reconciliation for reconcile, trusted-builder-construction
for construct, and system-bundles for other
ordinary workflows.
Secureboot-test runs the native EFI staging allocation regression before its
VM cases; that regression does not itself qualify firmware or boot.
Secureboot-test uses uefi-and-loader and records boot_verification as
qemu-qualification-requested.
Its system_image_verification field records the same requested qualification
for the authenticated preload; root_protection_verification remains
not-performed until the mandatory runtime boundary is implemented and tested.
Other workflows record boot_verification as not-performed.
All currently record root_protection_verification as not-performed.
These fields describe the workflow's verification coverage, not an observed
firmware state or a completed verification result.
Production malloc and signed bundles do not qualify verified boot or
protection against compromised root.
Subsequent records contain a sequence number, step name and exact argv array.
No commands are executed and no output directories are created by a plan.
This describes the current adapter invocation, not a completed artifact or
permission to execute installation steps.
The default format remains text.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd plan stage --signing development
build-bsd stage --signing development
build-bsd plan build --config /secure/bsd-build.conf
build-bsd build --incremental --jobs 8 --kernel GENERIC-NODEBUG
build-bsd packages --signing signed --key /secure/release.pem
build-bsd status /usr/obj/usr/src/build-runs/RUN
.Ed
.Ss Kernel module hints boundary
For media, build-bsd builds a host-native kldxref through the maintained
bootstrap Makefile in the run's native-tools directory and passes its absolute
path as HARDWARE_KLDXREF to release staging.
The backend requires an explicit regular executable and does not search PATH.
It records the generator and output hashes in a MODULE_HINTS_GENERATED log record
and fails if the generator changes during execution.
Direct release callers must supply HARDWARE_KLDXREF explicitly.
This records trusted-builder provenance, not authenticated runtime authority.
.Pp
Normal world and bootstrap builds include the matching
.Xr kldxref 8 .
It propagates detected module/record and hints-file errors instead of silently
accepting incomplete indexes.
Construction VM qualification requires explicit kldxref and real module inputs
and checks failures without root privileges.
It additionally stages seven synthetic ELF metadata objects built by the
maintained construction Makefile, checking PNP bounds, string-read errors,
fatal-path cleanup and legitimate absence of a metadata section.
The generator also provides an explicit-file mode with a fixed output path,
qualified by twenty additional VM checks.
It does not discover unselected files.
Construct's explicit module policy derives the file list from package inventory
and binds the generator, inputs and any replaced package output to their hashes.
Its VM fixture verifies actual jailed replacement and malformed-input rejection.
Directory filename discovery is not an authenticated module inventory.
Complete parser validation, authenticated policy and package provenance,
complete module-inventory coverage and generation sealing remain required
before a protected generation can be approved.
.Pp
The current BIP kernel refuses runtime module loading and unloading, including
capability-gated requests.
A generated module index does not establish that required drivers or service
facilities are available at boot.
Hardened qualification must establish that those facilities are built into the
selected kernel or authenticated and preloaded, then exercise them during normal
startup.
Package presence and index-generation success alone do not prove this coverage.
.Ss Certificate construction boundary
The maintained world and package builds include
.Xr certctl 8 .
Construct can derive certificate outputs from an explicit
.Fl -certificate-policy
input, with no default certificate discovery or legacy blacklist inputs.
The policy, resolved input selection, generator payload, output plan and
measured
result are recorded separately.
Generic construction commands allow at most 4096 arguments and 128 KiB of
encoded arguments including terminators; individual arguments remain bounded
at 4096 bytes.
.Pp
The construction VM checks the production compiler and actual jail execution
using every certificate payload in its explicit source archive, plus malformed
input rejection and an explicit trust-role change.
The archive bytes and selected executable dependencies are repackaged into a
synthetic fixture; this is not complete package installation or a signed
release.
Unprivileged utility regressions run separately.
Input authentication, complete code-loading closure, generation sealing and
protected runtime updates remain required.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr build 7 ,
.Xr capsign 8 ,
.Sh PUBLICATION DESTINATION
.Fl -publish-repository Ar directory
(configuration key
.Va publish_repository )
selects the final combined repository for a packages/build operation.
The default remains
.Pa /usr/obj/usr/src/repo .
A persistent destination such as
.Pa /var/db/5bsd/repositories/base
keeps published generations separate from disposable build output.
Base and hardware staging remain under
.Pa /usr/obj .
Only successful complete publication advances the destination's per-ABI
.Pa latest
pointer.
Creating or writing the destination requires normal filesystem
permissions; the tool does not relocate it on permission failure.

.Sh INSTALLED BOOT OBSERVATION
.Cm boot-observe
requires
.Fl -boot-observation-policy Ar file
and accepts
.Fl -output Ar directory .
The explicit JSON policy supplies hash-bound QEMU, firmware code, enrolled
firmware variables, boot media and data disks, plus CPU/memory limits,
observation duration and an optional loopback SSH forwarding port.
Policy format 2 additionally requires an explicit SSH probe: hash-bound client,
known-hosts file and private identity, guest account, remote command and expected
stdout digest. The probe uses strict host-key checking and bounded retries,
retaining each attempt. It disables automatic client-certificate discovery and
certificate algorithms, records the optional adjacent public-key file, and rejects
input paths containing SSH quoting or expansion tokens.
A requested probe must match before OBSERVED is possible;
a clean guest shutdown alone otherwise yields SSH_PROBE_FAILED.
This records one command observation, not complete SSH service qualification.
The current runner targets amd64 Q35 with firmware SMM enabled.
It boots the supplied media unchanged and read-only; firmware variables and
data disks are copied privately before use.
Networking is disabled unless explicitly requested, in which case restricted
user networking exposes only the requested loopback forwarding rule.
.Pp
This action collects serial output, QMP lifecycle events and input hashes.
The explicitly selected QEMU data directory must contain only physical
directories and regular files. Its names, sizes and file hashes are recorded
before launch and checked again after exit; changes invalidate the observation.
This records content identity, not publisher authentication or the complete
host execution environment.
It does not replace init or rc, inject a kernel command line, or infer a
successful login, SSH session or security qualification from boot output.
OBSERVED requires zero exit status and QMP evidence of guest poweroff; it is
a diagnostic result, not an acceptance PASS.
External termination or missing poweroff evidence is a harness failure.
Fatal serial markers are checked after exit as well as during observation.
A timeout records forced termination and exits 124; detected guest failures,
input changes and collector failures exit unsuccessfully.
See
.Pa /usr/src/tools/test/boot-observe/README.md
for the policy schema and limitations.

.Sh CONFIGURATION COMPOSITION
The
.Cm compose-config
action applies explicit top-level changes to an exact UCL base document.
Use
.Fl -configuration-base ,
.Fl -configuration-changes ,
.Fl -configuration-parser ,
.Fl -configuration-parser-sha256
and a fresh
.Fl -output
directory under /usr/obj.
The parser is the maintained bsd-ucl-json helper; its SHA256 is an explicit input.
The changes document contains format 1, base_sha256, replace, add and remove.
Replacement entries contain expected and value; removal entries contain the
expected prior value. Additions must name absent fields. Untouched fields are
preserved; nested objects and arrays are replaced whole. An empty grant is not
filled from defaults. Changes to the base require review and a new hash binding.
.Pp
The output contains configuration.json and receipt.json. This action does not
authenticate the base or validate service-specific semantics. Validate the
result with its consumer before including it as an explicit installation-policy
source in a new authenticated generation. It never edits the running system.

.Sh CAPSULE STATIC VALIDATION
The installed native helper
.Pa /usr/libexec/bsd-build/bsd-capsule-validate
shares Capsule's production configuration parser and effective-policy report.
It is statically linked and performs validation only; it neither starts a daemon
nor provisions storage. Select it explicitly with its measured SHA256.
For native build hosts, the maintained capimage/host-tools.mk target
.Cm bsd-capsule-validate
builds the same helper under the selected /usr/obj working directory.
It validates syntax and static constraints, not storage identities or bootability.
.Pp
For compose-config,
.Fl -capsule-validator Ar executable
and
.Fl -capsule-validator-sha256 Ar digest
select a matching Capsule executable with validation-only support.
They are optional for compose-config. Image storage bindings have been removed.
The builder invokes it with -n -f on the composed configuration before writing
the receipt. A validation failure leaves diagnostics and candidate configuration,
with no success receipt. The executable and output hashes must remain unchanged.
This validates syntax and Capsule's static constraints; it does not authenticate
policy, check live storage identity, grant runtime authority or activate boot.
The selected executable requires its matching runtime dependencies in the builder.

.Sh OFFLINE STATE STORAGE
.Cm state-storage
requires
.Fl -state-plan Ar FILE
and accepts a new
.Fl -output Ar DIRECTORY
under /usr/obj.
It executes only in an exclusive offline trusted builder with root privileges.
The builder may be a separate trusted virtual machine; root privileges are
required inside that builder, not necessarily on the machine hosting it.
The guest requires the matching /usr/src helper tree, Python 3.12, and the
explicitly selected native tools. Copying the frontend alone is insufficient.
Observe tool hashes and pool/dataset identities in the builder that executes
the plan. Export the pool and stop the builder before attaching its backing
disk to another guest; never attach the same writable disk concurrently.
The format-1 JSON plan names an operation, timeout in seconds, and explicit tools.
Each tool has path and sha256 fields; no host default tool is selected.
An allocate plan supplies dataset, pool_guid and parent_guid, with zfs, zpool
and mount tools. It creates a new empty child and records its GUID for subsequent
immutable policy construction.
An allocate plan may include an execution object with exec and setuid keys,
each containing the string on or off. Both default to off when the object is
omitted; setuid=on requires exec=on. This permits owner-selected ordinary
application storage without assigning integrity-management authority.
Device files remain disabled. The selected execution policy is recorded in the
allocation receipt and checked against dataset properties and mount options.
An initialize plan supplies a canonical source directory, observation and
allocation file references (each with path and sha256), plus the tar tool.
It verifies the recorded identity, offline properties and empty state before
copying the final reconciled seed. Execution policy is inherited from the allocation
receipt and cannot be overridden by the initialize plan. Legacy receipts without
that field retain exec=off,setuid=off. Existing data is not overwritten.
The verified allocation GUID is retained before temporary mountpoint setup.
If setup fails, cleanup restores the offline mountpoint only when a fresh GUID
check still identifies that allocation; it must not modify a replacement dataset.
Failure to query identity prevents safe cleanup and requires builder recovery.
Failure diagnostics record cleanup as not-attempted, completed,
refused-identity-mismatch or failed, with a separate cleanup error when applicable.
A cleanup failure preserves the initiating exception and never publishes a
successful provisioning receipt.
After the final pool sync, both operations recheck dataset identity, type,
mountpoint, mount state, canmount, devices, readonly and execution properties
before publishing a successful receipt.
Tool hashes are rechecked after these final operations.
A mismatch retains the dataset and failure diagnostics without a success receipt.
These are exclusive-builder observations, not protection against concurrent root.
Receipts remain unsigned construction evidence; this action does not sign,
activate, reboot or authorize runtime access.

.Sh INTEGRITY POLICY SCOPE
An explicitly ordinary mutable storage boundary may contain package
configuration and executable content.
Package membership does not grant protected runtime authority.
Declared protected consumer inputs must remain immutable, including aliases;
cross-boundary hardlinks remain prohibited.
For declared protected ELF consumers, construct and reconcile also require the
PT_INTERP reference to resolve to a reconciled immutable file through immutable
lookup components and aliases.
The report records the interpreter digest.
For BIP consumers it also rereads the interpreter without following filesystem
symlinks, checks its reconciled digest, and requires bounded ELF program headers
matching the explicit target class, byte order and machine.
No BIP feature mark is required on the interpreter itself.
This does not qualify kernel interpreter substitution, loader behavior or
transitive libraries.
Storage partition validation does not establish complete runtime dependency
coverage, authenticated boot, or protection against compromised root.

.Ss Storage policy capacity
Capsule and its shared native validator allocate storage tables for the selected
policy, bounded by 16 MiB of expanded entries per table and a 1 MiB input file.
The validator checks these limits before image construction; there is no fixed
eight-dataset layout limit. Static validation does not qualify descriptor or
mount acquisition in the running system.

.Sh CAPSULE VALIDATOR REPORT
The selected Capsule validator must support
.Fl n Fl j Fl f Ar configuration .
The maintained composition helper requires its versioned effective
service-manager report and bind that report to the validator and configuration
hashes.  A validator without this interface is rejected.
Recording the manager selection does not establish immutable dependency coverage
or runtime enforcement.

BIP format-3 image layouts and storage bindings are retired.
Old BIP layouts are rejected; their protection is not silently discarded.

.Ss Streaming verification read failures
The verifier preserves failed reads and premature EOF for known-size inputs;
finalization cannot turn these into success by retrying I/O. The native
verified-read-failure-test covers this behavior.
The kboot and OFW input callbacks also propagate allocation, mapping and read
failures as errors rather than EOF; readin-failure-test checks these cases with
injected operations. These native tests do not establish platform boot support. Generic backward replay and
unknown-length EOF handling require separate qualification.

.Ss Loader Lua read errors
The maintained lua-file-read-test checks byte counts, overflow, character reads
and persistent read errors in the loader Lua file wrapper. Required-verification
streams hash delivered bytes and finalize at close before normal script loading
returns; lua-stream-verify-test covers native mutation cases. Neither test executes
the Lua parser or firmware, and arbitrary consumers using data before close
remain outside this qualification.
The lua-config-io-test additionally exercises actual config.lua under a native
Lua interpreter, checking close-before-parse behavior and I/O failure handling;
its loader and file-I/O boundaries are injected.

.Ss EFI configuration verification
When firmware requires verification, the early EFI configuration reader checks
the retained buffer before parsing it and obtains size/type from the opened file.
The native efi-config-buffer-test covers changed bytes and error paths; it does
not qualify all loader script inputs or demonstrate firmware execution.

.Ss Boot observation serial processing
The maintained boot-observe runner scans appended serial bytes incrementally.
It retains fatal markers across polls and log replacement, handles split markers,
and bounds each scan to the log size observed at its start.
This reduces repeated I/O during long diagnostic runs.
It does not turn serial output or SSH port forwarding into service qualification.

.Ss Disk-backed integrity implementation status
The internal verity tree component has host-only construction and read-verification
tests and explicit checked-artifact integration with boot-stage.
This does not establish authenticated installed-root handoff or kernel admission.
No command or option currently qualifies this as a bootable hardened disk system.
The internal range verifier reuses one authenticated leaf hash block within
a request using bounded scratch space; every data block is still hashed.
Native tests and kernel-object compilation do not establish runtime performance.
An optional caller-bounded authenticated hash cache also has component tests.
Its provider concurrency and lifetime integration remain incomplete; no command
enables it as an installed-root backend.
The explicit verity_crypto_test kernel module tests OpenCrypto scatter/gather
SHA-256 requests; compile/link checks do not establish runtime correctness or
performance.
It is not installed, loaded or selected by normal build commands.
Its maintained instructions are in tools/test/construction/verity_crypto/README.
An accelerated verifier backend remains unimplemented.
An internal bounded builder can emit the combined filesystem and hash tree,
and boot-stage accepts explicit checked combined-image and descriptor inputs.
That input binding does not establish verified boot.

The loader has a bounded root-descriptor read path that verifies its private
buffer before copying it to load memory.
Kernel admission and full boot qualification remain incomplete.

Existing preload image results do not establish disk-backed system qualification.
Random-access streaming verifier contexts retain digests for completed pages and the bytes
of its partial final page. Backward reads compare the touched pages before
returning bytes; the whole-stream digest is still authenticated at close.
The native verified-replay regression covers altered full and partial replays,
restoration, page crossings, allocation failure and bounded replay I/O.
The non-seeking Lua stdio wrapper and streaming raw-file loader select an
enforced sequential restriction before reading. It allocates no replay table and rejects backward verified seeks;
whole-stream authentication at close remains mandatory.
Failed loads discard their verifier context and replay table without further
I/O or a verification success claim. File descriptors remain caller-owned.
For random-access contexts this adds one page hash per completed first-pass page and digest storage
proportional to file size. Loader memory and boot-time performance remain
unqualified. Authenticated consumption through every kernel, module and
root-descriptor load path remains an integrated qualification requirement.

.Ss Release trust rotation qualification
The maintained loader-trust-rotation-test builds native verifier profiles with
old-only, overlapping and new-only release anchors and tests real signatures,
foreign signer roles and explicit revocation.
It records public-anchor and build-artifact hashes.
These profiles do not attest an arbitrary EFI loader supplied to boot-stage.
The maintained EFI signer hashes nonempty sections in file-offset order;
uefi-digest-test and the staging signer regression check that calculation.
The existing boot-efi-signer input selects the signer executable and staging
records its digest. Signature-presence inspection is not cryptographic verification;
EFI signature and firmware verification remain explicitly separate results.
Removing an old anchor from a new loader does not revoke older signed loaders;
firmware enrollment/revocation, protected boot selection and rollback/recovery
remain separate integrated qualification requirements.

.Ss Explicit generation-trust loader build
The
.Cm loader-build
operation requires
.Fl loader-anchors Ar public-certificates.pem
and builds an unsigned EFI Lua loader using compiled generation trust anchors.
The PEM file may contain multiple public certificates for overlap during rotation;
private-key or mixed PEM inputs are rejected.
This operation currently supports native amd64 only and uses the requested
.Fl jobs
count (all logical CPUs by default).
.Pp
The output is a new directory below /usr/obj selected with
.Fl output .
It contains anchors.pem, inputs.json, build.log, an isolated obj tree and
loader-build.json recording the resulting executable and trust-header hashes.
The existing stand efi-loader target is used with firmware-db fallback for
manifest trust disabled and both make configuration files set to /dev/null.
Custom make configuration files and incremental builds are rejected for
this fixed trust profile. This does not change conventional loader builds.
.Pp
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd loader-build --loader-anchors /path/to/release-anchors.pem \
    --output /usr/obj/usr/src/loaders/release-candidate
.Ed
.Pp
This compiles software in a trusted builder; it does not independently attest
the source or toolchain. The receipt is not a release signature or proof of
firmware execution. Boot-stage requires the selected receipt and its SHA256
to bind the staged loader bytes. No firmware enrollment, signing, installation
or activation is performed by loader-build. Private signing keys are not inputs to this operation.

.Ss Binding an EFI staging input to its loader build
EFI signing through boot-stage requires
.Fl boot-loader-build Ar loader-build.json
and
.Fl boot-loader-build-sha256 Ar digest
in addition to the existing EFI inventory path and signing inputs.
Select the digest from the trusted builder output; it must not be treated as
independent source attestation merely because it accompanies a receipt.
.Pp
Staging verifies the pinned record, its supported compiled-trust profile, and
its loader size/hash against the staged inventory before invoking either signer.
It retains the exact selected record at efi/loader-build.json and records that
binding in stage.json. Missing, altered or mismatched inputs fail staging.
Paths named inside the receipt are not followed.
.Pp
This check binds artifacts to a selected build record. It does not establish
release-certificate authorization, independently attest compilation, or prove
firmware execution. Those results
remain separate and must not be inferred from staged_artifact_bound.

.Ss Paired native manifest verifier
The loader-build operation also builds loader-manifest-verify through its
maintained Makefile, with the same explicit public anchors and RSA/SHA256/SHA384
profile as the EFI loader. Both generated trust-header PEM payloads must match
the snapshotted public input. The build receipt records the native verifier's
path, executable digest and generated trust-header digest.
.Pp
The native tool accepts one manifest.sig path and uses the loader's actual
libsecureboot and BearSSL sources. It does not consult the system CA store or
import firmware trust. Its result concerns compiled generation anchors only;
it does not check firmware revocation, and certificate validity follows the
loader library's current default (expiry enforcement disabled).
EFI staging invokes the explicitly selected companion after checking its digest
against the pinned build record. Building it alone is not proof that a particular
staged generation is authorized.

.Ss Checking compiled generation trust during staging
EFI boot-stage additionally requires
.Fl boot-manifest-verifier Ar executable .
Its digest must match the companion verifier in the selected loader build
record. The path is explicitly selected; paths inside a receipt are never
executed implicitly.
.Pp
After generating the manifest signature, staging executes a checked private
snapshot of the verifier against the staged manifest, signature and certificates.
It checks their hashes before and after verification. Only an affirmative result
permits subsequent EFI signing and media publication. Rejection leaves no
successful stage.json. The result records compiled_generation_trust_verified;
firmware revocation, certificate expiry enforcement and firmware execution remain
separate unchecked properties. This verifies a signature under the selected
compiled anchors, not organizational approval or independent build attestation.

.Ss EFI signing payload preservation
The boot-stage backend checks that signing preserves every byte of the selected
unsigned loader except its PE checksum and certificate-directory fields.
The certificate table must be appended and cover exactly the remaining output.
This construction profile rejects pre-signed loader inputs.
The streaming comparison uses bounded buffers and adds no runtime I/O checks.
A successful transformation records executable_payload_preserved=true;
it does not establish cryptographic signature validity or firmware acceptance.

.Ss Independent EFI signature verification
EFI boot-stage requires boot-efi-signature-verifier, an explicitly selected
osslsigncode executable. After signing and payload preservation checks, staging
verifies Authenticode using the selected certificate and its exact DER leaf hash.
The signature_verified receipt field becomes true only after this check.
Firmware trust, boot success and revocation remain unverified by this operation.
The tools are trusted builder inputs, not executables chosen by a build receipt.

.Ss Root transition artifact construction
A verity-mode boot-policy JSON object may contain a transition object with
exactly these fields: version (1), transport_fs (ufs or zfs), transport_device,
provider, workers, cache_blocks, window_bytes, and buffer_bytes.
All selections and resource bounds are explicit inputs.
The image pathname comes from the outer boot policy; the descriptor digest comes
from checked generation artifacts.
Staging generates /boot/root-transition.bin and includes it in the manifest.
The bounded versioned record contains data only, with no mount options or scripts.
Transport and image strings must fit 1024-byte NUL-terminated fields, and the
provider name must fit 64 bytes.
Workers are a positive 32-bit count; cache entries are nonnegative, and window
and buffer sizes are positive signed-64-bit-range integers.
These encoding checks do not establish target MAXCPU, maxphys, block alignment
or sufficient provider allocation budget.
.Pp
The loader supports authenticated transition selection and retained preload
checks. Staging itself is construction evidence: its receipt reports
loader_consumed=false, kernel_admission_enabled=false and
target_resource_limits_verified=false. It does not enable disk-root boot.
The hardened kernel continues to require its existing immutable preloaded root.

.Ss Retained root transition handoff
When a transition is selected, the generated Lua contract also binds its path
and SHA256. The loader authenticates its exact bytes before switching to the
verified image and checks that its image path and descriptor digest match the
selection. It retains that private record and preloads it with the descriptor.
Final kernel handoff rejects missing, duplicate, changed or unselected transition
records. An older loader without the extended selection command cannot boot this
contract; a matching newly built loader is required. Staging requires root_transition_version=1 in the pinned build receipt for
this contract. Missing or unsupported declarations fail before either signer.
Kernel transition consumption remains unfinished.
No staging result implies firmware execution or completed disk-root admission.

.Ss Loader transition compatibility
The maintained loader-build profile records root_transition_version=1 in its
inputs and successful build receipt. Staging binds that declaration to the pinned
receipt and the selected loader bytes. A record without this declaration remains
usable only for contracts that do not require the transition artifact.
This is a trusted-builder compatibility statement, not independent source or
compiler attestation, firmware execution evidence, or activation authorization.

.Sh QUALIFICATION STATUS
BIP has been dropped. Its storage, session-policy, principal and provider-fixture
options have been removed from this frontend. The legacy Secure Boot harness
still requires separation from retired BIP fixtures; its historical results do
not qualify the current source tree. Independent Secure Boot and veriexec
remain supported development objectives, not completed qualification claims.

.Sh FIRMWARE QUALIFICATION
.Cm secureboot-test Fl test-case Cm firmware
runs only the independent amd64 UEFI firmware suite.
It requires explicit
.Fl test-firmware-code
and
.Fl test-firmware-vars
paths for the secure OVMF code and setup-mode variable template.
No staged operating-system root is required or accepted.
The existing
.Fl qemu , Fl qemu-data , Fl uefi-vars-tool
options select VM tools.
.Fl test-openssl , Fl test-uefisign , Fl test-makefs , Fl test-mkimg
can override native host tool paths.
The suite generates disposable keys in its private output directory, copies
all variable stores, and runs one 2048 MiB VM at a time.
It checks setup-mode execution, trusted execution, and rejection of unsigned,
untrusted-signer and modified images.
It also tests certificate revocation through an offline-generated dbx store:
the revoked signer must fail while a second trusted signer continues to work.
This proves boot-time revocation enforcement, not authenticated variable updates.
The probe uses the maintained EFI startup code and build libraries; preparatory
loader compilation is not loader execution qualification.
This suite does not qualify authenticated key rotation, recovery, the operating
system loader, veriexec, system startup or protection against root.

The suite audits exported variable stores before starting guests.
Only the intended dbx certificate append may differ; both enrolled certificates
must remain in db and the databases must retain authenticated-write attributes.

The authenticated-update client also tests a KEK-signed db replacement,
unauthorized updates and replay rejection through firmware SetVariable.
The replacement signer must be rejected before the update and accepted on a
subsequent boot; the retired signer must then be rejected.
Only public payloads and signatures are copied to the VM; private keys stay
in the host test output directory.
This does not qualify PK/KEK rotation, recovery or OS runtime update tooling.

A subsequent fresh guest replays the original db update against the persisted
store and must reject it. A newly signed update with a later timestamp must
succeed, and replaying that newer update must also be rejected.

.Ss Retired BIP loader fixtures
The protected dynamic-loader fixtures and their build inputs have been removed.
The legacy combined Secure Boot runner rejects BIP kernel qualification; it
still contains other retired dependencies and is not an operational OS test.
Use the independent firmware test case for its documented firmware-only scope.
This does not qualify the complete loader, kernel and installed-system chain.

