.Ss Freestanding boot toolchains
Format-5 source graph inputs may select target-kind set to standalone.
This requires freestanding toolchain schema 1 in a freshly staged SDK receipt.
The native SDK supports AMD64, ARM64 and RISC-V target platforms independently
of the native userland platform used to build and execute tools.
Compilation defaults to no standard include search and freestanding semantics;
links default to no standard libraries.
Compiler and linker runtime dependencies remain declared.
Source owners must declare any required compiler resource or system headers.
The complete SDK inventory is still verified before and after qualification.
Older SDKs are rejected for this mode, rather than using a userland toolchain.
Only declared host artifact tests may execute in this mode.
This is a component qualification interface; Make remains the production backend.
.Ss Managed Bazel action caching
Format-5 source inputs may supply an action-cache object with name, max-bytes,
max-age-seconds, idle-delay-seconds and min-free-bytes.
Its private builder-owned directory is derived below the standard object state;
symlinks and shared permissions are rejected.
Omitting the object or using null disables persistent action caching.
This cache is separate from repository-cache, which supplies module downloads.
Size and age are idle garbage-collection targets; free-space reserve is a
pre-build check, not a hard peak disk quota.
The optional build-state identifier selects a private named output state while
sharing the managed action cache and verified SDK inputs.
Physical installation trees are excluded from content-cache restoration;
canonical archives encode link identities and installation metadata.
Existing physical outputs still require verification before packaging.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/MANAGED-ACTION-CACHE-QUALIFICATION.md .
.Ss Mixed hosted and freestanding targets
A format-5 userland selection may consume freestanding artifacts through the
maintained bsd_freestanding_artifact dependency transition.
The dependency selects its standalone SDK platform.
The source module registers the disjoint SDK toolchains;
execution tools retain the native execution configuration.
Source-graph inputs require snapshot-bound freestanding toolchain schema 2.
Regenerate older SDKs with the maintained SDK stager.
The wrapper forwards files, output groups and typed installation providers.
It does not forward C/C++ linkage providers or executable-tool interfaces.
The EFI packages expose freestanding_image and freestanding_install_manifest
for hosted image and installation consumers.
The architecture defaults to the consumer CPU and may be explicitly selected
for a multi-architecture image.
These interfaces do not establish complete world or package qualification.

.Ss IA32 boot compilation
The standalone SDK includes an i386 platform for 32-bit BIOS and IA32 EFI
producer graphs, with native AMD64 execution tools.
This is a nested artifact architecture, not a new supported system target.
The ABI boundary accepts architecture i386 explicitly.
Schema 2 adds this platform; regenerate older SDK inputs with the maintained
SDK stager before source qualification.
Compilation and linking checks inspect ELF32 artifacts without executing them.
Complete BIOS and IA32 EFI producer graphs remain separate qualification work.
.Ss IA32 EFI support library qualification
The selected libefi32 component uses the shared libefi source inventory with
