Public object and crt0 aliases cross the standalone artifact boundary.
Separate debug output is selected through the preserved debug output group.
The host artifact test checks ELF32 relocatable identity, startup position,
platform symbols and debug-link integrity; boot2 remains a checked consumer.
These construction checks do not execute BIOS firmware.
.Ss Complete BIOS Lua construction
The default text-only BIOS Lua contribution uses thirty-six source-owned entry
objects, the standalone BTX startup object and the regular Lua, BIOS platform
and standalone archives in the original link order.
The BTX kernel and loader stub also use explicit i386 artifact boundaries.
The loader_bios_max_size integer setting defaults to Make's 505000-byte client
limit; the actual strip action rejects overflow before publishing output.
The loader_bios_address integer setting defaults to 2097152 and controls the
stub origin and final a.out entry through the same configured value.
The strip recipes remove a stale debug link inherited from the separately split
startup object; this static client has no separate program debug file.
The contribution installs /boot/loader_lua with its default-loader and zfsloader
hardlinks.  Its real archive can join an archive set across the ABI boundary,
which preserves archive metadata providers without forwarding CcInfo.
The selected profile requires text-only Lua, normal disk/network/MD/GELI/ZFS
support and the regular verifier disabled. Unsupported profiles are incompatible.
Construction and archive tests do not execute firmware or qualify a full system.
.Ss Standalone legacy BIOS construction
The boot0, serial boot0, MBR, protective MBR and cdboot contributions compile
with the standalone i386 toolchain and link through the declared SDK linker.
Their public image and object labels cross the freestanding artifact boundary;
boot images are data rather than hosted executable tools.
The existing legacy installation manifest/archive preserves image and manual
metadata without a special frontend mode.
Selected-default Make comparisons and host format checks do not qualify
non-default boot policy, firmware execution or full-system performance.
.Ss GPT and ISO BIOS boot construction
The gptboot and isoboot contributions use standalone i386 compiler contexts,
explicit startup/program/serial/disk/console object order and native libsa32.
Declared SDK linking, flat conversion and native BTX packing construct the images.
The existing loader_comconsole_port and loader_comconsole_speed settings select
